const QUESTIONS = [
  {"n":1,"topic":2,"q":1,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  HOTSPOT<br><br>Overview<br />\nContoso Ltd. is a software development company located in the United States.<br />\nExisting Environment<br />\nGitHub Environment<br />\nContoso uses GitHub Enterprise and assigns GitHub Copilot Pro+ licenses to its developers. The developers use Microsoft Visual Studio Code as their IDE.<br />\nContoso has a customer portal. The code for the portal is stored in a GitHub repository named repo1 that contains the following:<br />\nA custom agent named agent1 that includes instructions to review specs related to best practices<br />\nA custom instruction file named validate-instructions.md that is used to validate tone of voice and applies to all .md and .txt files<br />\nA custom instruction file named codereview.instructions.md that is used by the Copilot coding agent but is excluded for use by the Copilot code review<br />\nrepo1 has the following structure:<br />\nThe front-end is stored in the /frontend folder.<br />\nThe API logic is stored in the /api folder.<br />\nContoso has a second repository named repo2 that contains a legacy .NET application named App1 built by using .NET 6. repo2 has a multi-agent workflow for modernization tasks.<br />\nContoso enables the Model Context Protocol (MCP) registry and allows the Microsoft Learn MCP Server. Every developer must configure their own connection to the Learn MCP Server.<br />\nProblem Statements<br />\nThe developers working in repo1 report that the Microsoft Learn documentation is NOT being retrieved when they attempt to validate a design by using agent1.<br />\nThe testing team at Contoso identifies that the customer portal uses inconsistent UI styles, which leads to customer confusion and branding issues. The UI inconsistencies stem from variations in the folder structure.<br />\nAgent Logs<br />\nYou have the following logs for the multi-agent workflow used in repo2.<br />\n<img title=\"image1\" src=\"images/image1.png\"><br />\nRequirements<br />\nPlanned Changes<br />\nContoso plans to have all agents and developers in repo1 use the Microsoft Learn MCP to ensure that reviews are validated by using the appropriate documentation. This must be implemented centrally.<br />\nContoso plans to leverage AI-powered coding agents to implement new portal features and pages.<br />\nTechnical Requirements<br />\nApp1 must be upgraded to .NET 10. A previous upgrade attempt was started by using the Copilot modernization agent, but the attempt was never finalized.<br />\nYou plan to retry the upgrade. You must first analyze App1 by using AI, and then generate a report that contains breaking changes and deprecated patterns before retrying the upgrade.<br />\nAll AI-generated code for UI styling must adhere to a predefined folder structure.<br />\nThe architects at Contoso need help building implementation plans for repo1. The company wants to implement a new agent named agent2 to analyze the code base and the code requirements, and then respond with a detailed plan. The agent must NOT be able to edit files or run local commands.<br />\nThe developers must be able to delegate work to the Copilot coding agent by assigning issues to the agent.<br />\nYou need to implement agent2 to meet the technical requirements.<br />\nHow should you complete the YAML configuration? To answer, drag the appropriate values to the correct targets. Each value may be used once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.<br />\n<br />\n<img title=\"image2\" src=\"images/image2.png\"><br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<img src=\"images/image3.png\" alt=\"official answer\"><span class=\"whead\">Proposed answers:</span><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"atbl\"><tr><th>#</th><th>Target</th><th>Value</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>First blank, right after <code>tools: [</code> (first dropdown)</td><td><b>'search',</b></td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Second blank (second dropdown, below the first)</td><td><b>'read',</b></td></tr></table></div><span class=\"whead\">Result:</span><pre class=\"acode\">---\nname: implementation-planner\ndescription: Creates detailed implementation plans and technical specifications in markdown format\ntools: [\n<mark>  'search',      &lt;- blank 1</mark>\n<mark>  'read',        &lt;- blank 2</mark>\n  'microsoftdocs/mcp/docs_search',\n  'microsoftdocs/mcp/docs_fetch'\n]\n---</pre><span class=\"whead\">Why:</span><ul><li><b><code>tools</code> list</b>: it is an allow-list: the agent can only do what is listed.</li><li><b><code>search</code></b>: lets the planner find code in the repo, needed to analyze the code base.</li><li><b><code>read</code></b>: lets it open files and requirements, so it can write a detailed plan.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Why not the others:</span><ul><li><b>'agent',</b>: dropdown 1 option: would let the planner delegate to other agents; not needed for a plan.</li><li><b>'edit',</b>: dropdown 1 option: would let it change files, which is forbidden.</li><li><b>'execute',</b>: dropdown 2 option: would let it run local commands, which is forbidden.</li><li><b>'web',</b>: dropdown 2 option: not needed; Learn docs come from the two <code>microsoftdocs</code> tools.</li></ul><div class=\"learnbox\"><span class=\"whead\">Learn it:</span><ul><li><b>Concept</b>: <code>tools</code> is the allow-list in a custom agent file (<code>.github/agents/name.agent.md</code>). <code>search</code> finds code, <code>read</code> opens files, <code>edit</code> changes files, <code>execute</code> runs terminal commands, <code>agent</code> lets it call sub-agents, <code>web</code> fetches web pages. Entries like <code>microsoftdocs/mcp/docs_search</code> are tools from an MCP server (a plug-in that gives the agent extra abilities).</li><li><b>How it works</b>: The YAML front matter (between the <code>---</code> lines) sets the name, description and tools. The agent can only use tools on the list; anything not listed is unavailable. Plain text below the front matter is the instructions.</li><li><b>Example</b>: A \"planner\" agent: <code>tools: ['search', 'read']</code> can explore the repo and write a plan, but cannot change a single file or run a command.</li><li><b>Exam tip</b>: \"Must NOT edit files or run commands\" means leave out <code>edit</code> and <code>execute</code>. Trap: adding <code>agent</code> or <code>web</code> because they sound useful; they are not needed.</li></ul></div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/agent-customization/custom-agents\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Custom agents in VS Code (tools, handoffs)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/copilot/customization/mcp-servers\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MCP servers in VS Code</a></li></ul>"},
  {"n":2,"topic":2,"q":2,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  Case study<br><br>Overview<br />\nContoso Ltd. is a software development company located in the United States.<br />\nExisting Environment<br />\nGitHub Environment<br />\nContoso uses GitHub Enterprise and assigns GitHub Copilot Pro+ licenses to its developers. The developers use Microsoft Visual Studio Code as their IDE.<br />\nContoso has a customer portal. The code for the portal is stored in a GitHub repository named repo1 that contains the following:<br />\nA custom agent named agent1 that includes instructions to review specs related to best practices<br />\nA custom instruction file named validate-instructions.md that is used to validate tone of voice and applies to all .md and .txt files<br />\nA custom instruction file named codereview.instructions.md that is used by the Copilot coding agent but is excluded for use by the Copilot code review<br />\nrepo1 has the following structure:<br />\nThe front-end is stored in the /frontend folder.<br />\nThe API logic is stored in the /api folder.<br />\nContoso has a second repository named repo2 that contains a legacy .NET application named App1 built by using .NET 6. repo2 has a multi-agent workflow for modernization tasks.<br />\nContoso enables the Model Context Protocol (MCP) registry and allows the Microsoft Learn MCP Server. Every developer must configure their own connection to the Learn MCP Server.<br />\nProblem Statements<br />\nThe developers working in repo1 report that the Microsoft Learn documentation is NOT being retrieved when they attempt to validate a design by using agent1.<br />\nThe testing team at Contoso identifies that the customer portal uses inconsistent UI styles, which leads to customer confusion and branding issues. The UI inconsistencies stem from variations in the folder structure.<br />\n<br />\nAgent Logs<br />\nYou have the following logs for the multi-agent workflow used in repo2.<br />\n<img title=\"image4\" src=\"images/image1.png\"><br />\nRequirements<br />\nPlanned Changes<br />\nContoso plans to have all agents and developers in repo1 use the Microsoft Learn MCP to ensure that reviews are validated by using the appropriate documentation. This must be implemented centrally.<br />\nContoso plans to leverage AI-powered coding agents to implement new portal features and pages.<br />\nTechnical Requirements<br />\nApp1 must be upgraded to .NET 10. A previous upgrade attempt was started by using the Copilot modernization agent, but the attempt was never finalized.<br />\nYou plan to retry the upgrade. You must first analyze App1 by using AI, and then generate a report that contains breaking changes and deprecated patterns before retrying the upgrade.<br />\nAll AI-generated code for UI styling must adhere to a predefined folder structure.<br />\nThe architects at Contoso need help building implementation plans for repo1. The company wants to implement a new agent named agent2 to analyze the code base and the code requirements, and then respond with a detailed plan. The agent must NOT be able to edit files or run local commands.<br />\nThe developers must be able to delegate work to the Copilot coding agent by assigning issues to the agent.<br />\nBefore App1 is upgraded, you need to verify each individual upgrade step and whether all tests have passed.<br />\nWhich file should you use?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tassessment.md<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tplan.md<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tagent.md<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\ttasks.md<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<span class=\"whead\">Proposed answers:</span><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"atbl\"><tr><th>Question part</th><th>Answer</th></tr><tr><td>Correct answer</td><td><b>D. tasks.md</b></td></tr></table></div><span class=\"whead\">Why:</span><ul><li><b><code>tasks.md</code></b>: the live checklist: every upgrade step and its status (including build/test results) is recorded here.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Why not the others:</span><ul><li><b>A. assessment.md</b>: the analysis report (breaking changes) written before the upgrade; it has no step status.</li><li><b>B. plan.md</b>: the strategy in prose; it does not track execution.</li><li><b>C. agent.md</b>: not a modernization artifact.</li></ul><div class=\"note\"><b>Note:</b> The saved official key lists A, but the Microsoft upgrade docs describe <code>tasks.md</code> as the per-task tracker, so this quiz teaches D.</div><div class=\"learnbox\"><span class=\"whead\">Learn it:</span><ul><li><b>Concept</b>: The Copilot app modernization agent upgrades old apps (e.g. .NET 6 to .NET 10) in stages. <code>assessment.md</code> = report of breaking changes and deprecated patterns. <code>plan.md</code> = the upgrade strategy. <code>tasks.md</code> = the step-by-step checklist with status. <code>agent.md</code> is not one of these files.</li><li><b>How it works</b>: Files are written under <code>.github/upgrades/&lt;scenario&gt;/</code>. Order: assess, then plan (plan + tasks), then execute; each task is built and tested, and its status is updated in <code>tasks.md</code>.</li><li><b>Example</b>: Task list: <code>[x] Update project files</code>, <code>[ ] Run all tests</code>. Open it to see which step is done and whether tests passed.</li><li><b>Exam tip</b>: Keywords \"verify each individual step\" and \"tests passed\" = per-step status = <code>tasks.md</code>. Trap: <code>assessment.md</code> sounds like checking, but it is only the pre-upgrade analysis. The saved key says A, so know both.</li></ul></div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/porting/github-copilot-app-modernization/concepts\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">App modernization: assessment, plan, tasks.md</a></li><li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/porting/github-copilot-upgrade/overview\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot upgrade: how the stages work</a></li></ul>"},
  {"n":3,"topic":2,"q":3,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  DRAG DROP<br />\nYou have a GitHub repository that has a GitHub Actions workflow. The workflow runs an AI agent.<br />\nYou need to ensure that the default GITHUB_TOKEN permissions are read-only, and write access is granted to only the job that performs repository write operations. The workflow must be able to create and approve pull requests only when explicitly enabled.<br />\nHow should you complete the workflow? To answer, drag the appropriate values to the correct targets. Each value may be used once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.<br />\n<br />\n<img title=\"image5\" src=\"images/image4.png\"><br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<img src=\"images/image5.png\" alt=\"official answer\"><span class=\"whead\">Proposed answers:</span><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"atbl\"><tr><th>#</th><th>Target</th><th>Value</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td><code>permissions:</code> at the top of the workflow (blank under it)</td><td><b>contents: read</b></td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td><code>permissions:</code> under the <code>update_artifacts:</code> job (blank under it)</td><td><b>contents: write</b></td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Step <code>- name: Create pull request</code> (blank above <code>run: echo \"create PR\"</code>)</td><td><b>pull-requests: write</b></td></tr></table></div><span class=\"whead\">Result:</span><pre class=\"acode\">name: agent-pr\non:\n  pull_request:\npermissions:\n<mark>  contents: read</mark>\njobs:\n  analyze:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v5\n      - name: Read-only analysis\n        run: echo \"Analyze PR content and post a comment via API\"\n  update_artifacts:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    permissions:\n<mark>      contents: write</mark>\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v5\n      - name: Update generated artifacts\n        run: |\n          echo \"regenerate\"\n      - name: Create pull request\n<mark>        pull-requests: write</mark>\n        run: echo \"create PR\"</pre><span class=\"whead\">Why:</span><ul><li><b><code>contents: read</code> at top</b>: every job gets read-only by default (least privilege).</li><li><b><code>contents: write</code> in the job</b>: only <code>update_artifacts</code> regenerates and commits files, so only it gets write.</li><li><b><code>pull-requests: write</code></b>: needed to create/approve PRs, and it is given only at the step that does that.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Why not the others:</span><ul><li><b>issues: write</b>: nothing in the workflow touches issues.</li><li><b>permissions: read-all</b>: a shortcut for read on every scope; the top-level default asked for here is <code>contents: read</code>.</li><li><b>pull-requests: read</b>: read-only cannot create or approve pull requests.</li></ul><div class=\"learnbox\"><span class=\"whead\">Learn it:</span><ul><li><b>Concept</b>: <code>GITHUB_TOKEN</code> is a temporary token every workflow run gets to call the GitHub API. <code>permissions</code> limits what it can do: <code>contents</code> (files/commits), <code>pull-requests</code> (create/comment/approve PRs), <code>issues</code> (issues). Each can be <code>read</code>, <code>write</code> or <code>none</code>. <code>read-all</code> / <code>write-all</code> are shortcuts for all scopes.</li><li><b>How it works</b>: <code>permissions</code> can sit at workflow level (applies to all jobs) or job level (overrides for that job). Once you list any scope, every scope you do not list becomes <code>none</code>. Org/repo settings also set the default token access, and a separate setting controls whether Actions may create and approve PRs.</li><li><b>Example</b>: Top: <code>permissions: contents: read</code>. Writer job: <code>permissions: contents: write</code> plus <code>pull-requests: write</code>. Read-only jobs stay safe.</li><li><b>Exam tip</b>: \"Default read-only, write only for one job\" = top-level <code>contents: read</code> + job-level write. Trap: <code>read-all</code> sounds like read-only but is broader than needed; <code>issues: write</code> is a distractor when issues are not mentioned.</li></ul></div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/actions/security-for-github-actions/security-guides/automatic-token-authentication\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">GITHUB_TOKEN permissions</a></li></ul>"},
  {"n":4,"topic":2,"q":4,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  DRAG DROP<br />\nYou have a GitHub repository that uses the GitHub Copilot coding agent to resolve issues and create draft pull requests. The repository uses GitHub Actions for CI, and reviewers rely on pull request timelines and workflow artifacts to understand what the agent did.<br />\nDuring long-running agent tasks, the reviewers lose track of decisions and validation steps, which causes repeated questions and reworks when context drifts between iterations.<br />\nYou need to persist task progress and decisions as durable artifacts and ensure that the reviewers can verify what the agent did during and after execution by using GitHub as the system of record.<br />\nWhat should you do for each requirement? To answer, drag the appropriate actions to the correct requirements. Each action may be used once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.<br />\n<br />\n<img title=\"image7\" src=\"images/image6.png\"><br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<img src=\"images/image7.png\" alt=\"official answer\"><span class=\"whead\">Proposed answers:</span><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"atbl\"><tr><th>#</th><th>Target</th><th>Value</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Create durable run outputs that are downloadable after completion:</td><td><b>Use the upload-artifact action and configure artifact retention in the CI workflow.</b></td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Provide ongoing, human-reviewable task progress signals inside the pull request:</td><td><b>Assign an issue and wait for the agent to view the issue.</b></td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Provide a real-time view of agent actions for audit and troubleshooting:</td><td><b>Select View session to stream live agent logs.</b></td></tr></table></div><span class=\"whead\">Why:</span><ul><li><b>upload-artifact + retention</b>: saves files that stay downloadable after the run ends.</li><li><b>Assign an issue</b>: starts the agent, which then posts its progress on the pull request for reviewers (per the official answer image).</li><li><b>View session</b>: streams the live agent log, so you can audit and troubleshoot while it works.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Why not the others:</span><ul><li><b>Select Approve and run workflows in the pull request merge box.</b>: a manual gate for CI, not a progress record.</li><li><b>Mention @copilot in the merged pull request to restart the agent.</b>: restarts the agent; it records nothing.</li><li><b>Watch for pull request body updates and pull request timeline events.</b>: sounds right for slot 2, but the official answer image places \"Assign an issue…\" there.</li></ul><div class=\"note\"><b>Note:</b> The old explanation put \"PR body and timeline\" in the middle slot; the official answer image shows \"Assign an issue and wait for the agent to view the issue\", so that is kept.</div><div class=\"learnbox\"><span class=\"whead\">Learn it:</span><ul><li><b>Concept</b>: <b>Artifacts</b> are files saved from a workflow run (via <code>actions/upload-artifact</code>) and downloaded later. <b>Retention</b> is how many days they are kept. <b>View session</b> is the button on a Copilot coding agent pull request that shows the agent's live log. The <b>PR timeline</b> is the list of events on a pull request.</li><li><b>How it works</b>: Assigning an issue to Copilot makes it create a branch and a draft PR and keep updating the PR description with progress. Artifacts are set in YAML with <code>retention-days</code>. Session logs stream while the agent works.</li><li><b>Example</b>: <code>- uses: actions/upload-artifact@v4</code> with <code>name: agent-report</code>, <code>path: report.md</code>, <code>retention-days: 30</code>.</li><li><b>Exam tip</b>: Match the verb: \"durable/downloadable\" = artifact; \"real-time audit/troubleshoot\" = View session. Trap: actions that start, approve or restart the agent do not record progress.</li></ul></div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/actions/using-workflows/storing-workflow-data-as-artifacts\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Workflow artifacts</a></li><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/coding-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot coding agent</a></li></ul>"},
  {"n":5,"topic":2,"q":5,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  DRAG DROP<br />\nYou have a GitHub Enterprise Cloud Organization that uses the GitHub Copilot coding agent to resolve issues asynchronously.<br />\n<br />\nWhen an issue is assigned to GitHub Copilot, the agent creates a draft pull request, but your team cannot always tell whether the agent is actively working, has completed its session, or is awaiting workflow approval.<br />\nWhich execution context does each signal indicate? To answer, drag the appropriate context to the correct signals. Each signal may be used once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.<br />\n<br />\n<img title=\"image9\" src=\"images/image8.png\"><br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<img src=\"images/image9.png\" alt=\"official answer\"><span class=\"whead\">Proposed answers:</span><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"atbl\"><tr><th>#</th><th>Target</th><th>Value</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>The eyes emoji (eyes icon) reaction appears on the issue:</td><td><b>The agent acknowledges the assignment and will create the draft pull request.</b></td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>The pull request timeline shows Copilot started work:</td><td><b>The agent session is actively running and generating live logs.</b></td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>A draft pull request exists, but GitHub Actions checks are not running:</td><td><b>A human must manually approve and run the workflows.</b></td></tr></table></div><span class=\"whead\">Why:</span><ul><li><b>Eyes reaction</b>: Copilot saying \"got it, I accepted this issue\".</li><li><b>Timeline \"started work\"</b>: the session is live and producing logs.</li><li><b>Checks not running</b>: workflows on agent PRs wait until a human clicks <b>Approve and run</b>.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Why not the others:</span><ul><li><b>The agent session timed out and must be reassigned.</b>: nothing in these signals says the session ended.</li><li><b>The agent cannot see the repository content due to exclusions.</b>: exclusions do not make checks sit idle.</li><li><b>The agent comment was ignored because the user lacks write access.</b>: no comment is involved in these signals.</li></ul><div class=\"learnbox\"><span class=\"whead\">Learn it:</span><ul><li><b>Concept</b>: An <b>eyes emoji</b> on an issue is Copilot's \"I saw this and I'm starting\" signal. The <b>timeline</b> events on the PR show the session starting. <b>Approve and run workflows</b> is a button that appears when a workflow run needs a human OK before it executes.</li><li><b>How it works</b>: Copilot coding agent PRs are treated like PRs from outside contributors: Actions workflows do not run until someone with write access approves them. So the PR exists but checks show as waiting/not running.</li><li><b>Example</b>: You assign an issue, see the eyes reaction, then a draft PR appears with \"Copilot started work\" in the timeline; checks wait until you click <b>Approve and run workflows</b>.</li><li><b>Exam tip</b>: \"Draft PR exists but checks are not running\" = needs manual approval. Trap: assuming the agent failed or timed out; it is only waiting for you.</li></ul></div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/coding-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot coding agent</a></li></ul>"},
  {"n":6,"topic":2,"q":6,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You have a repository that uses the GitHub Copilot coding agent and supports hooks stored under .github/hooks.<br />\nYou need a Shell command to run automatically whenever an agent execution fails.<br />\nWhich type of hook should you use?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tpostToolUse<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tsessionEnd<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\terrorOccurred<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tagentStop<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<span class=\"whead\">Proposed answers:</span><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"atbl\"><tr><th>Question part</th><th>Answer</th></tr><tr><td>Correct answer</td><td><b>C. errorOccurred</b></td></tr></table></div><span class=\"whead\">Why:</span><ul><li><b><code>errorOccurred</code></b>: fires only when an agent execution fails, which is exactly the trigger asked for.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Why not the others:</span><ul><li><b>A. postToolUse</b>: runs after every tool call, success or not.</li><li><b>B. sessionEnd</b>: runs when the session ends, even on success.</li><li><b>D. agentStop</b>: about the agent stopping, not about a failure.</li></ul><div class=\"learnbox\"><span class=\"whead\">Learn it:</span><ul><li><b>Concept</b>: <b>Hooks</b> are shell commands that Copilot runs at set points (events). Common events: <code>sessionStart</code>, <code>sessionEnd</code>, <code>userPromptSubmitted</code>, <code>preToolUse</code> (before a tool runs, can block it), <code>postToolUse</code> (after a tool runs), <code>errorOccurred</code> (when an error happens). Check the current docs for the exact event list.</li><li><b>How it works</b>: Hooks are configured in JSON files under <code>.github/hooks/</code>. Each hook names an event and a command (bash/PowerShell) to run. The event data is passed to the command as JSON.</li><li><b>Example</b>: <code>errorOccurred</code> hook running a script that posts a Slack alert whenever the agent errors.</li><li><b>Exam tip</b>: Match the trigger word in the question (\"fails\" = <code>errorOccurred</code>). Trap: <code>sessionEnd</code>/<code>postToolUse</code> run even when nothing failed.</li></ul></div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/copilot-cli\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot CLI</a></li></ul>"},
  {"n":7,"topic":2,"q":7,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You are about to start a complex refactoring task in the GitHub Copilot CLI.<br />\nBefore Copilot makes any changes, you need to review and agree on the approach.<br />\nWhat should you do first?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tStart the Copilot CLI and specify the --agent=Task parameter.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tStart the Copilot CLI and specify the --allow-all parameter.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tFrom the Copilot CLI, switch to plan mode.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tFrom the Copilot CLI, run the /compact command.<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<span class=\"whead\">Proposed answers:</span><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"atbl\"><tr><th>Question part</th><th>Answer</th></tr><tr><td>Correct answer</td><td><b>C. From the Copilot CLI, switch to plan mode.</b></td></tr></table></div><span class=\"whead\">Why:</span><ul><li><b>Plan mode</b>: Copilot researches and proposes an approach, then waits for you to approve it before changing anything.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Why not the others:</span><ul><li><b>A. Start the Copilot CLI and specify the --agent=Task parameter.</b>: just picks an agent; no review step.</li><li><b>B. Start the Copilot CLI and specify the --allow-all parameter.</b>: skips approvals, the opposite of reviewing first.</li><li><b>D. From the Copilot CLI, run the /compact command.</b>: only shrinks the conversation context.</li></ul><div class=\"learnbox\"><span class=\"whead\">Learn it:</span><ul><li><b>Concept</b>: <b>Plan mode</b> in Copilot CLI (switch with Shift+Tab or <code>/plan</code>) makes Copilot analyze the task and write a plan for you to review before any code is changed. <code>--allow-all</code> auto-approves all permissions. <code>--agent</code> chooses a custom agent. <code>/compact</code> summarizes the conversation to save context.</li><li><b>How it works</b>: In plan mode Copilot asks clarifying questions, produces a step list, and waits. You approve or edit it, then it implements.</li><li><b>Example</b>: Before a big refactor, start in plan mode, review \"1. extract interface, 2. update callers, 3. add tests\", then approve.</li><li><b>Exam tip</b>: \"Review and agree on the approach before changes\" = plan mode. Trap: <code>--allow-all</code> does the opposite (removes confirmations).</li></ul></div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/copilot-cli\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot CLI</a></li></ul>"},
  {"n":8,"topic":2,"q":8,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  A team assigns an issue to the GitHub Copilot coding agent by using the following one-line description: Fix the login bug.<br />\nCopilot creates a pull request, but the pull request is missing changes and has an incorrect scope.<br />\nHow should you resolve the issue?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tEnable Copilot memory.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tUpdate the Model Context Protocol (MCP) server rate limits.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tAdd a clear description of the problem to the issue.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tAllocate more resources to copilot-setup-steps.yml.<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<span class=\"whead\">Proposed answers:</span><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"atbl\"><tr><th>Question part</th><th>Answer</th></tr><tr><td>Correct answer</td><td><b>C. Add a clear description of the problem to the issue.</b></td></tr></table></div><span class=\"whead\">Why:</span><ul><li><b>Issue text</b>: the coding agent plans from the issue; \"Fix the login bug\" gives no scope, so the PR is incomplete and mis-scoped.</li><li><b>Clear description</b>: states the problem, expected result and how to verify, so the agent can scope correctly.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Why not the others:</span><ul><li><b>A. Enable Copilot memory.</b>: personalizes style; cannot invent missing scope.</li><li><b>B. Update the Model Context Protocol (MCP) server rate limits.</b>: affects tool/doc calls, not PR scope.</li><li><b>D. Allocate more resources to copilot-setup-steps.yml.</b>: fixes the runner environment, not a vague issue.</li></ul><div class=\"learnbox\"><span class=\"whead\">Learn it:</span><ul><li><b>Concept</b>: The <b>Copilot coding agent</b> works on a GitHub issue by itself and opens a PR. Its input is the issue text plus repo files and custom instructions. <b>Copilot memory</b> stores learned facts about a repo. <b>MCP</b> servers give the agent extra tools. <code>copilot-setup-steps.yml</code> is the workflow that prepares the agent's environment (install dependencies, tools).</li><li><b>How it works</b>: The agent can only act on what it is told and can find. A vague issue gives a vague plan; a good issue states the problem, expected behavior, acceptance criteria and relevant files.</li><li><b>Example</b>: Better issue: \"Login form on /signin returns 500 when password has a +. Expected: login succeeds. Touch only <code>auth/</code>. Add a unit test.\"</li><li><b>Exam tip</b>: Symptom = \"missing changes / wrong scope\" with a one-line issue: fix the input (the issue). Trap: tooling options (memory, MCP limits, setup resources) do not fix unclear requirements.</li></ul></div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/coding-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot coding agent</a></li></ul>"},
  {"n":9,"topic":3,"q":1,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  Case study<br><br>Existing Environment<br />\nGitHub Environment<br />\nThe GitHub environment contains the following:<br />\nThree repositories named product-api, billing-service, and infra-terraform.<br />\nBranch protection on the main branch in all repositories that requires at least one pull request review before merging<br />\nGitHub Actions runners used across all workflows<br />\nA GitHub team named SG_Dev that contains developers<br />\nA GitHub team named SG_Review that contains senior engineers and a security team<br />\nA .github/copilot-instructions.md file that includes general coding conventions for all features<br />\nAgent environment<br />\nThe product-api repository uses a GitHub Copilot coding agent named agent1 that has the following configurations:<br />\nNo custom agent profile is defined.<br />\nA Model Context Protocol (MCP) server named MCP1 is deployed to https://mcp.litwareinc.internal and provides access to internal ticketing and deployment APIs. MCP1 requires an API key for authentication.<br />\nA second Copilot coding agent named agent2 handles changes in infra-terraform and runs in parallel with agent1 when both agents have open assigned issues.<br />\nCopilot memory is NOT enabled for the organization.<br />\nProblem Statements<br />\nLitware identifies the following issues:<br />\nDuring two recent sessions, agent1 accessed files in billing-service, which is outside the agent's intended scope.<br />\nagent1 makes code changes immediately after receiving a task.<br />\nA developer named Ben, who is on the SG_Dev team, reports that agent1 completed a session with a successful status and opened a pull request, but the pull request contains no file changes. Other developers report this intermittently as well.<br />\nBoth agent1 and agent2 modified shared/config.yaml in a parallel test run, generating conflicting outputs.<br />\nagent1 consistently uses raw try-catch blocks for error handling, which violates the defined implementation guidelines of SG_Dev.<br />\nRequirements<br />\nPlanned Changes<br />\nLitware plans to make the following changes:<br />\nEnsure that agent1 can access all the tools in the environment.<br />\nProvide product-api with specific instructions to agent1 without affecting Copilot Chat or Copilot code review.<br />\nConfigure MCP1 as a tool for agent1 by modifying the product-api repository MCP configuration.<br />\nEnsure that Copilot retains details that it has learned and uses that knowledge for future work. This must be applied to all licensed members of the organization.<br />\nImplementation guidelines<br />\nThe development team at Litware identifies the following implementation guidelines:<br />\nAgent workflows must be able to run in parallel.<br />\nApplication error handling must use the repository ErrorHandler class.<br />\nagent1 and agent2 must run on isolated branches during parallel execution. File-level conflicts must be detected before merges, and both agents must be able to run concurrently.<br />\nSecurity requirements<br />\nLitware identifies the following security requirements:<br />\nOnly the members of SG_Review must be able to approve agent1 plan outputs.<br />\nAll API keys must be stored and accessed securely.<br />\nThe developers must NOT be able to self-approve.<br />\n<br />\nAgent configuration<br />\n<img title=\"image11\" src=\"images/image10.png\"><br />\nYou need to configure agent1 to support the planned changes.<br />\nWhat should you do?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tDelete line 05 from the agent configuration.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tAdd Use all available tools to the .github/copilot-instructions.md file.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tAdd Use all available tools to the instructions in the agent configuration.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tAdd the mcp-servers property to the agent configuration.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>E.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tIn the agent configuration, replace line 05 with the following.05 tools: [].<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<span class=\"whead\">Proposed answers:</span><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"atbl\"><tr><th>Question part</th><th>Answer</th></tr><tr><td>Correct answer</td><td><b>A. Delete line 05 from the agent configuration.</b></td></tr></table></div><span class=\"whead\">Result:</span><pre class=\"acode\">01 ---\n02 name: agent1\n03 description: Coordinates code changes for the product-api repository using approved tools and conventions.\n<mark>05 tools: ['read', 'search', 'edit']      &lt;- delete this line</mark>\n06 model: gpt-5.2\n07 user-invokable: true\n08 disable-model-invocation: false\n09 ---\n10 You are agent1 for the product-api repository.</pre><span class=\"whead\">Why:</span><ul><li><b>Line 05</b>: <code>tools</code> is an allow-list limiting agent1 to read, search, edit.</li><li><b>Deleting it</b>: with no <code>tools</code> key, the agent gets all available tools, matching the planned change.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Why not the others:</span><ul><li><b>B. Add Use all available tools to the .github/copilot-instructions.md file.</b>: prose is only guidance, grants no tools, and would also affect Chat and code review.</li><li><b>C. Add Use all available tools to the instructions in the agent configuration.</b>: prose inside the profile likewise grants nothing.</li><li><b>D. Add the mcp-servers property to the agent configuration.</b>: MCP1 is configured through the repo MCP configuration, a separate planned change.</li><li><b>E. In the agent configuration, replace line 05 with the following. 05 tools: [].</b>: an empty list gives no tools, the opposite.</li></ul><div class=\"note\"><b>Note:</b> Some community answers pick D; the official key and case study logic say A.</div><div class=\"learnbox\"><span class=\"whead\">Learn it:</span><ul><li><b>Concept</b>: A <b>custom agent profile</b> is a <code>.agent.md</code> file with YAML front matter. <code>tools</code> restricts which tools the agent may use. <code>mcp-servers</code> in a profile can define MCP servers for that agent. <b>Instructions</b> files are only guidance text.</li><li><b>How it works</b>: If <code>tools</code> is omitted, the agent can use all available tools; if it is listed, only those are allowed; <code>tools: []</code> means none. Text like \"use all tools\" does not change permissions.</li><li><b>Example</b>: Remove <code>tools: ['read','search','edit']</code> and the agent gets everything, including MCP tools.</li><li><b>Exam tip</b>: \"Access all tools\" = delete/omit the <code>tools</code> restriction. Trap: instructions text and <code>tools: []</code> look plausible but do not grant access. Remember the case study wires MCP1 via the repo MCP configuration, which is a separate change.</li></ul></div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/agent-customization/custom-agents\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Custom agents in VS Code (tools, handoffs)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/copilot/customization/mcp-servers\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MCP servers in VS Code</a></li></ul>"},
  {"n":10,"topic":3,"q":2,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  Case study<br><br>Existing Environment<br />\nGitHub Environment<br />\nThe GitHub environment contains the following:<br />\nThree repositories named product-api, billing-service, and infra-terraform.<br />\nBranch protection on the main branch in all repositories that requires at least one pull request review before merging<br />\nGitHub Actions runners used across all workflows<br />\nA GitHub team named SG_Dev that contains developers<br />\nA GitHub team named SG_Review that contains senior engineers and a security team<br />\nA .github/copilot-instructions.md file that includes general coding conventions for all features<br />\nAgent environment<br />\nThe product-api repository uses a GitHub Copilot coding agent named agent1 that has the following configurations:<br />\nNo custom agent profile is defined.<br />\nA Model Context Protocol (MCP) server named MCP1 is deployed to https://mcp.litwareinc.internal and provides access to internal ticketing and deployment APIs. MCP1 requires an API key for authentication.<br />\nA second Copilot coding agent named agent2 handles changes in infra-terraform and runs in parallel with agent1 when both agents have open assigned issues.<br />\nCopilot memory is NOT enabled for the organization.<br />\nProblem Statements<br />\nLitware identifies the following issues:<br />\nDuring two recent sessions, agent1 accessed files in billing-service, which is outside the agent's intended scope.<br />\nagent1 makes code changes immediately after receiving a task.<br />\nA developer named Ben, who is on the SG_Dev team, reports that agent1 completed a session with a successful status and opened a pull request, but the pull request contains no file changes. Other developers report this intermittently as well.<br />\nBoth agent1 and agent2 modified shared/config.yaml in a parallel test run, generating conflicting outputs.<br />\nagent1 consistently uses raw try-catch blocks for error handling, which violates the defined implementation guidelines of SG_Dev.<br />\nRequirements<br />\nPlanned Changes<br />\nLitware plans to make the following changes:<br />\nEnsure that agent1 can access all the tools in the environment.<br />\nProvide product-api with specific instructions to agent1 without affecting Copilot Chat or Copilot code review.<br />\nConfigure MCP1 as a tool for agent1 by modifying the product-api repository MCP configuration.<br />\nEnsure that Copilot retains details that it has learned and uses that knowledge for future work. This must be applied to all licensed members of the organization.<br />\nImplementation guidelines<br />\nThe development team at Litware identifies the following implementation guidelines:<br />\nAgent workflows must be able to run in parallel.<br />\nApplication error handling must use the repository ErrorHandler class.<br />\nagent1 and agent2 must run on isolated branches during parallel execution. File-level conflicts must be detected before merges, and both agents must be able to run concurrently.<br />\nSecurity requirements<br />\nLitware identifies the following security requirements:<br />\nOnly the members of SG_Review must be able to approve agent1 plan outputs.<br />\nAll API keys must be stored and accessed securely.<br />\nThe developers must NOT be able to self-approve.<br />\n<br />\nAgent configuration<br />\n<img title=\"image12\" src=\"images/image10.png\"><br />\nYou need to provide access to the API key of MCP1. The solution must meet the security requirements.<br />\nWhat should you do?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tStore the API key as a GitHub Codespaces user secret scoped to product-api.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tStore the API key as a secret in the Copilot environment of product-api by using a name prefix of COPILOT_MCP_, and then reference the variable name in the mcp.json configuration.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tIn the product-api repository settings, add the API key directly to the .mcp/server.json file by using a plaintext apiKey field.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tIn product-api, add the API key as a GitHub Actions encrypted secret and reference the secret by using ${{ secrets.KEY }} in the workflow YAML of agent1.<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<span class=\"whead\">Proposed answers:</span><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"atbl\"><tr><th>Question part</th><th>Answer</th></tr><tr><td>Correct answer</td><td><b>B. Store the API key as a secret in the Copilot environment of product-api by using a name prefix of COPILOT_MCP_, and then reference the variable name in the mcp.json configuration.</b></td></tr></table></div><span class=\"whead\">Result:</span><pre class=\"acode\">\"apiKey\": \"<mark>$COPILOT_MCP_MCP1</mark>\"</pre><span class=\"whead\">Why:</span><ul><li><b><code>COPILOT_MCP_</code> secret</b>: the only secret type the coding agent's MCP runtime can read.</li><li><b>Reference by name</b>: <code>mcp.json</code> holds only the variable name, so the key is never committed.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Why not the others:</span><ul><li><b>A. Store the API key as a GitHub Codespaces user secret scoped to product-api.</b>: only available in Codespaces, not to the coding agent.</li><li><b>C. In the product-api repository settings, add the API key directly to the .mcp/server.json file by using a plaintext apiKey field.</b>: leaks the key to anyone with repo access.</li><li><b>D. In product-api, add the API key as a GitHub Actions encrypted secret and reference the secret by using ${{ secrets.KEY }} in the workflow YAML of agent1.</b>: normal Actions secrets are not passed to the agent's MCP servers.</li></ul><div class=\"learnbox\"><span class=\"whead\">Learn it:</span><ul><li><b>Concept</b>: The Copilot coding agent runs in its own <b>copilot environment</b> in repo settings (Settings, Environments). Secrets or variables there whose names start with <code>COPILOT_MCP_</code> are passed to MCP servers. <b>Actions secrets</b> are for workflows; <b>Codespaces secrets</b> are for Codespaces.</li><li><b>How it works</b>: The MCP config (JSON in repo settings, Copilot, coding agent) references the value by name, e.g. <code>\"env\": {\"API_KEY\": \"$COPILOT_MCP_API_KEY\"}</code>. The real key is never in the repo.</li><li><b>Example</b>: Create a secret <code>COPILOT_MCP_MCP1_KEY</code> in the copilot environment and reference <code>$COPILOT_MCP_MCP1_KEY</code> in the MCP config.</li><li><b>Exam tip</b>: Keywords \"API key\", \"securely\", \"MCP\", \"coding agent\" = <code>COPILOT_MCP_</code> secret in the copilot environment. Trap: plaintext keys in files and ordinary Actions secrets.</li></ul></div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/copilot/customization/mcp-servers\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MCP servers in VS Code</a></li><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/coding-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot coding agent</a></li></ul>"},
  {"n":11,"topic":3,"q":3,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  HOTSPOT<br><br>Existing Environment<br />\nGitHub Environment<br />\nThe GitHub environment contains the following:<br />\nThree repositories named product-api, billing-service, and infra-terraform.<br />\nBranch protection on the main branch in all repositories that requires at least one pull request review before merging<br />\nGitHub Actions runners used across all workflows<br />\nA GitHub team named SG_Dev that contains developers<br />\nA GitHub team named SG_Review that contains senior engineers and a security team<br />\nA .github/copilot-instructions.md file that includes general coding conventions for all features<br />\nAgent environment<br />\nThe product-api repository uses a GitHub Copilot coding agent named agent1 that has the following configurations:<br />\nNo custom agent profile is defined.<br />\nA Model Context Protocol (MCP) server named MCP1 is deployed to https://mcp.litwareinc.internal and provides access to internal ticketing and deployment APIs. MCP1 requires an API key for authentication.<br />\nA second Copilot coding agent named agent2 handles changes in infra-terraform and runs in parallel with agent1 when both agents have open assigned issues.<br />\nCopilot memory is NOT enabled for the organization.<br />\nProblem Statements<br />\nLitware identifies the following issues:<br />\nDuring two recent sessions, agent1 accessed files in billing-service, which is outside the agent's intended scope.<br />\nagent1 makes code changes immediately after receiving a task.<br />\nA developer named Ben, who is on the SG_Dev team, reports that agent1 completed a session with a successful status and opened a pull request, but the pull request contains no file changes. Other developers report this intermittently as well.<br />\nBoth agent1 and agent2 modified shared/config.yaml in a parallel test run, generating conflicting outputs.<br />\nagent1 consistently uses raw try-catch blocks for error handling, which violates the defined implementation guidelines of SG_Dev.<br />\nRequirements<br />\nPlanned Changes<br />\nLitware plans to make the following changes:<br />\nEnsure that agent1 can access all the tools in the environment.<br />\nProvide product-api with specific instructions to agent1 without affecting Copilot Chat or Copilot code review.<br />\nConfigure MCP1 as a tool for agent1 by modifying the product-api repository MCP configuration.<br />\nEnsure that Copilot retains details that it has learned and uses that knowledge for future work. This must be applied to all licensed members of the organization.<br />\nImplementation guidelines<br />\nThe development team at Litware identifies the following implementation guidelines:<br />\nAgent workflows must be able to run in parallel.<br />\nApplication error handling must use the repository ErrorHandler class.<br />\nagent1 and agent2 must run on isolated branches during parallel execution. File-level conflicts must be detected before merges, and both agents must be able to run concurrently.<br />\nSecurity requirements<br />\nLitware identifies the following security requirements:<br />\nOnly the members of SG_Review must be able to approve agent1 plan outputs.<br />\nAll API keys must be stored and accessed securely.<br />\nThe developers must NOT be able to self-approve.<br />\n<br />\nAgent configuration<br />\n<img title=\"image13\" src=\"images/image10.png\"><br />\nYou are evaluating how agent1 will behave after you implement the planned changes.<br />\nFor each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.<br />\n<br />\n<img title=\"image14\" src=\"images/image11.png\"><br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<span class=\"whead\">Proposed answers:</span><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"atbl\"><tr><th>#</th><th>Statement</th><th>Answer</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Once MCP1 is configured, agent1 can invoke MCP1 tools autonomously.</td><td><b>Yes</b></td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>agent1 can use the MCP resources and MCP prompts exposed by MCP1.</td><td><b>No</b></td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>If new tools are added to MCP1, they will be available automatically to agent1.</td><td><b>Yes</b></td></tr></table></div><span class=\"whead\">Why:</span><ul><li><b>Statement 1 – Yes</b>: configured MCP tools are callable by the agent on its own.</li><li><b>Statement 3 – Yes</b>: the server advertises its tool list each session, so new tools appear without config changes.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Why not the others:</span><ul><li><b>Statement 2 – \"agent1 can use the MCP resources and MCP prompts exposed by MCP1.\" is No</b>: the coding agent uses MCP <i>tools</i>; resources and prompts are separate MCP features that are not enabled here.</li></ul><div class=\"note\"><b>Note:</b> Statement 2 is debated in the community; this answer follows the official key.</div><div class=\"learnbox\"><span class=\"whead\">Learn it:</span><ul><li><b>Concept</b>: <b>MCP</b> (Model Context Protocol) servers can offer <b>tools</b> (actions the AI can call), <b>resources</b> (data to read) and <b>prompts</b> (reusable prompt templates). Copilot coding agent supports MCP <b>tools</b> only.</li><li><b>How it works</b>: The agent discovers a server's tool list when it starts a session, so tools added to the server later show up without editing the config. It calls allowed tools on its own, since the coding agent runs without asking.</li><li><b>Example</b>: MCP1 adds a <code>create_ticket</code> tool: agent1 can call it. A \"ticket template\" prompt or a ticket resource would not be usable.</li><li><b>Exam tip</b>: Statements about \"resources and prompts\" with the coding agent = No; about tools = Yes. Trap: assuming every MCP feature is supported everywhere.</li></ul></div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/copilot/customization/mcp-servers\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MCP servers in VS Code</a></li><li><a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/agent-customization/custom-agents\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Custom agents in VS Code</a></li></ul>"},
  {"n":12,"topic":3,"q":4,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  Case study<br><br>Overview<br />\nContoso Ltd. is a software development company located in the United States.<br />\nExisting Environment<br />\nGitHub Environment<br />\nContoso uses GitHub Enterprise and assigns GitHub Copilot Pro+ licenses to its developers. The developers use Microsoft Visual Studio Code as their IDE.<br />\nContoso has a customer portal. The code for the portal is stored in a GitHub repository named repo1 that contains the following:<br />\nA custom agent named agent1 that includes instructions to review specs related to best practices<br />\nA custom instruction file named validate-instructions.md that is used to validate tone of voice and applies to all .md and .txt files<br />\nA custom instruction file named codereview.instructions.md that is used by the Copilot coding agent but is excluded for use by the Copilot code review<br />\nrepo1 has the following structure:<br />\nThe front-end is stored in the /frontend folder.<br />\nThe API logic is stored in the /api folder.<br />\nContoso has a second repository named repo2 that contains a legacy .NET application named App1 built by using .NET 6. repo2 has a multi-agent workflow for modernization tasks.<br />\nContoso enables the Model Context Protocol (MCP) registry and allows the Microsoft Learn MCP Server. Every developer must configure their own connection to the Learn MCP Server.<br />\nProblem Statements<br />\nThe developers working in repo1 report that the Microsoft Learn documentation is NOT being retrieved when they attempt to validate a design by using agent1.<br />\nThe testing team at Contoso identifies that the customer portal uses inconsistent UI styles, which leads to customer confusion and branding issues. The UI inconsistencies stem from variations in the folder structure.<br />\n<br />\nAgent Logs<br />\nYou have the following logs for the multi-agent workflow used in repo2.<br />\n<img title=\"image16\" src=\"images/image1.png\"><br />\nRequirements<br />\nPlanned Changes<br />\nContoso plans to have all agents and developers in repo1 use the Microsoft Learn MCP to ensure that reviews are validated by using the appropriate documentation. This must be implemented centrally.<br />\nContoso plans to leverage AI-powered coding agents to implement new portal features and pages.<br />\nTechnical Requirements<br />\nApp1 must be upgraded to .NET 10. A previous upgrade attempt was started by using the Copilot modernization agent, but the attempt was never finalized.<br />\nYou plan to retry the upgrade. You must first analyze App1 by using AI, and then generate a report that contains breaking changes and deprecated patterns before retrying the upgrade.<br />\nAll AI-generated code for UI styling must adhere to a predefined folder structure.<br />\nThe architects at Contoso need help building implementation plans for repo1. The company wants to implement a new agent named agent2 to analyze the code base and the code requirements, and then respond with a detailed plan. The agent must NOT be able to edit files or run local commands.<br />\nThe developers must be able to delegate work to the Copilot coding agent by assigning issues to the agent.<br />\nYou need to make changes to repo1 to support the planned changes for the agents.<br />\nWhat should you modify?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\t.github/agents/*.agent.md<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\t.vscode/settings.json<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\t.vscode/mcp.json<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\t&lt;project&gt;/.mcp/server.json<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<span class=\"whead\">Proposed answers:</span><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"atbl\"><tr><th>Question part</th><th>Answer</th></tr><tr><td>Correct answer</td><td><b>C. .vscode/mcp.json</b></td></tr></table></div><span class=\"whead\">Why:</span><ul><li><b><code>.vscode/mcp.json</code></b>: a workspace file you commit to the repo, so every developer and agent gets the Learn MCP server automatically (central setup).</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Why not the others:</span><ul><li><b>A. .github/agents/*.agent.md</b>: describes one agent's persona, not MCP servers.</li><li><b>B. .vscode/settings.json</b>: personal local settings, not central.</li><li><b>D. &lt;project&gt;/.mcp/server.json</b>: project-scoped coding-agent config, not repo-wide.</li></ul><div class=\"learnbox\"><span class=\"whead\">Learn it:</span><ul><li><b>Concept</b>: <code>.vscode/mcp.json</code> is the workspace file listing MCP servers for VS Code. <code>settings.json</code> holds editor settings. <code>.agent.md</code> defines a single custom agent. <code>.mcp/server.json</code> is not the standard repo-wide VS Code location.</li><li><b>How it works</b>: Because <code>.vscode/mcp.json</code> is in the repo, everyone who opens it in VS Code gets the same servers (VS Code asks to trust/start them). Developers can still add personal servers in their user profile.</li><li><b>Example</b>: <code>{ \"servers\": { \"learn\": { \"type\": \"http\", \"url\": \"https://learn.microsoft.com/api/mcp\" } } }</code></li><li><b>Exam tip</b>: \"Centrally for all developers and agents in the repo\" = committed workspace file. Trap: user-level settings are per person, not central.</li></ul></div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/copilot/customization/mcp-servers\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MCP servers in VS Code</a></li></ul>"},
  {"n":13,"topic":3,"q":5,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You have a GitHub repository.<br />\nYou use the GitHub Copilot CLI to run an agentic workflow from the terminal.<br />\nDuring execution, the conversation history approaches the context limit. The agent cannot continue the current session unless the amount of retained context is reduced.<br />\nYou need to continue the current session without losing all the prior progress.<br />\nWhich Copilot CLI slash command should you run?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\t/context<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\t/yolo<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\t/clear<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\t/compact<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<span class=\"whead\">Proposed answers:</span><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"atbl\"><tr><th>Question part</th><th>Answer</th></tr><tr><td>Correct answer</td><td><b>D. /compact</b></td></tr></table></div><span class=\"whead\">Why:</span><ul><li><b><code>/compact</code></b>: summarizes the conversation so the session continues with the key conclusions in less context.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Why not the others:</span><ul><li><b>A. /context</b>: only shows what is loaded; frees nothing.</li><li><b>B. /yolo</b>: auto-approves tools; unrelated to context.</li><li><b>C. /clear</b>: wipes the history, losing all prior progress.</li></ul><div class=\"learnbox\"><span class=\"whead\">Learn it:</span><ul><li><b>Concept</b>: The <b>context window</b> is how much conversation the model can hold at once. <code>/compact</code> summarizes history to free space. <code>/context</code> shows context usage. <code>/clear</code> starts fresh, discarding history. <code>/yolo</code> turns on auto-approval of tool permissions.</li><li><b>How it works</b>: Copilot CLI can also auto-compact near the limit. After <code>/compact</code> the session continues with a short summary instead of the full transcript.</li><li><b>Example</b>: After a long debug session: run <code>/compact</code>, then keep going with the fix.</li><li><b>Exam tip</b>: \"Reduce context but keep progress\" = <code>/compact</code>. Trap: <code>/clear</code> frees space but loses progress; <code>/context</code> only shows it.</li></ul></div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/copilot-cli\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot CLI</a></li></ul>"},
  {"n":14,"topic":3,"q":6,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You have a GitHub Enterprise repository.<br />\nAn agent opens pull requests to the main branch.<br />\nYou need to ensure that changes to .github/workflows/* and /infra/* require approval from designated reviewers before merge.<br />\nWhat should you configure?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\ta ruleset and an agents.md file<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\ta ruleset and a .copilotignore file<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\ta branch protection rule and a CODEOWNERS file<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\ta branch protection rule and copilot-instructions.md<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<span class=\"whead\">Proposed answers:</span><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"atbl\"><tr><th>Question part</th><th>Answer</th></tr><tr><td>Correct answer</td><td><b>C. a branch protection rule and a CODEOWNERS file</b></td></tr></table></div><span class=\"whead\">Result:</span><pre class=\"acode\">.github/workflows/*  @org/platform-team\n/infra/*             @org/security-team</pre><span class=\"whead\">Why:</span><ul><li><b>Branch protection rule</b>: the lock: requires reviews (including code owner review) before merging to main.</li><li><b><code>CODEOWNERS</code></b>: names the designated reviewers for <code>.github/workflows/*</code> and <code>/infra/*</code>.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Why not the others:</span><ul><li><b>A. a ruleset and an agents.md file</b>: agent guidance only; enforces no approval.</li><li><b>B. a ruleset and a .copilotignore file</b>: <code>.copilotignore</code> only hides files from Copilot.</li><li><b>D. a branch protection rule and copilot-instructions.md</b>: guides the agent; cannot force a human review.</li></ul><div class=\"note\"><b>Note:</b> The CODEOWNERS lines above are an example; team names are made up.</div><div class=\"learnbox\"><span class=\"whead\">Learn it:</span><ul><li><b>Concept</b>: <b>Branch protection rules</b> (or <b>rulesets</b>) enforce merge conditions like required reviews. <b>CODEOWNERS</b> maps file paths to people/teams who must review them. <code>.copilotignore</code>, <code>copilot-instructions.md</code> and <code>agents.md</code> are Copilot-related files; they do not enforce human approval.</li><li><b>How it works</b>: Put CODEOWNERS in <code>.github/CODEOWNERS</code> (or root or <code>docs/</code>). Then enable \"Require review from Code Owners\" in the protection rule. Later patterns win in the file.</li><li><b>Example</b>: <code>/infra/ @org/platform-team</code> means any PR touching <code>/infra/</code> needs that team's approval before merge.</li><li><b>Exam tip</b>: \"Specific paths need approval from designated reviewers\" = CODEOWNERS + protection rule. Trap: Copilot files influence the agent but cannot block merges. Rulesets can also require code owner review, but CODEOWNERS is still needed to name the reviewers.</li></ul></div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/configuring-branches-and-merges-in-your-repository/managing-protected-branches/about-protected-branches\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Branch protection rules</a></li><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/managing-your-repositorys-settings-and-features/customizing-your-repository/about-code-owners\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CODEOWNERS</a></li></ul>"},
  {"n":15,"topic":3,"q":7,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  HOTSPOT<br />\nYou have a GitHub repository that uses the following GrtHub Copilot CLI command in a Bash script.<br />\n<img title=\"image17\" src=\"images/image13.png\"><br />\nFor each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.<br />\n<br />\n<img title=\"image18\" src=\"images/image14.png\"><br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<span class=\"whead\">Proposed answers:</span><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"atbl\"><tr><th>#</th><th>Statement</th><th>Answer</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>The command will prevent runaway loops.</td><td><b>Yes</b></td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>The command will allow the agent to use all tools.</td><td><b>Yes</b></td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>The command will allow targeted human intervention at key decision points.</td><td><b>No</b></td></tr></table></div><span class=\"whead\">Result:</span><pre class=\"acode\">#!/usr/bin/env bash\nset -euo pipefail\nPROMPT=\"Update dependencies, run tests, and open a pull request if changes are needed.\"copilot --autopilot --yolo <mark>--\nmax-autopilot-continues 10</mark> -p \"$PROMPT\"</pre><span class=\"whead\">Why:</span><ul><li><b><code>--max-autopilot-continues 10</code></b>: hard cap on autonomous rounds, so it cannot loop forever.</li><li><b><code>--yolo</code></b>: pre-approves every tool, so all tools are usable.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Why not the others:</span><ul><li><b>Statement 3 – \"The command will allow targeted human intervention at key decision points.\" is No</b>: <code>--autopilot</code> never asks and <code>--yolo</code> approves everything, so nobody is prompted at decision points.</li></ul><div class=\"note\"><b>Note:</b> The prompt string and <code>copilot</code> are joined without a space in the question image; treat it as a typo.</div><div class=\"learnbox\"><span class=\"whead\">Learn it:</span><ul><li><b>Concept</b>: <code>--autopilot</code> lets Copilot CLI keep working without waiting for you. <code>--yolo</code> auto-approves all tool and path permissions (same idea as allow-all). <code>--max-autopilot-continues N</code> limits how many times autopilot continues. <code>-p</code> passes a prompt non-interactively. <code>set -euo pipefail</code> makes the Bash script stop on errors.</li><li><b>How it works</b>: In a script nobody is watching, so the cap is the safety net: after N continuations the run stops. With <code>--yolo</code>, no permission prompts appear, so a human is never asked.</li><li><b>Example</b>: <code>copilot --autopilot --yolo --max-autopilot-continues 10 -p \"$PROMPT\"</code> runs up to 10 continuations then ends.</li><li><b>Exam tip</b>: Look at each flag: a cap = prevents runaway loops; yolo = all tools; autopilot + yolo = no human check points. Trap: thinking the cap gives human intervention; it just stops the run.</li></ul></div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/copilot-cli\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot CLI</a></li></ul>"},
  {"n":16,"topic":3,"q":8,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  HOTSPOT<br />\nYou have a GitHub repository that uses GitHub Actions for CI.<br />\nYour team is piloting the GitHub Copilot coding agent to autonomously create branches and open pull requests. The repository follows trunk-based development that uses main as the default branch.<br />\nYou need to ensure that the agent meets the following requirements:<br />\nChanges to main can occur only by using pull requests that have at least one approval.<br />\nWhen a pull request is opened, a validation workflow runs, and the agent can still create branches and open pull requests autonomously.<br />\nHow should you configure the repository? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.<br />\n<br />\n<img title=\"image20\" src=\"images/image16.png\"><br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<span class=\"whead\">Proposed answers:</span><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"atbl\"><tr><th>#</th><th>Target</th><th>Value</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Branch protection rule (main) - Require pull request before merging:</td><td><b>Enabled</b></td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Workflow trigger for validation checks:</td><td><b>on: pull_request</b></td></tr></table></div><span class=\"whead\">Result:</span><pre class=\"acode\"># Branch protection rule (main)\nRequire pull request before merging: <mark>Enabled</mark>\n\n# Validation workflow\non:\n  <mark>pull_request:</mark></pre><span class=\"whead\">Why:</span><ul><li><b>Enabled</b>: branch protection with required PR + approval blocks direct pushes to <code>main</code>; the agent still creates branches and opens PRs, so it is not blocked.</li><li><b>on: pull_request</b>: the validation workflow runs on every PR the agent opens or updates, before merge.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Why not the others:</span><ul><li><b>Blank 1 – Disabled</b>: no PR is required, so direct pushes to <code>main</code> are allowed.</li><li><b>Blank 1 – Enabled with bypass for direct pushes</b>: bypass lets someone push straight to <code>main</code>.</li><li><b>Blank 2 – on: push</b>: runs after a push, it does not gate PRs.</li><li><b>Blank 2 – on: schedule</b>: runs on a timer, not tied to a PR.</li><li><b>Blank 2 – on: workflow_dispatch</b>: manual run only, not triggered by a PR.</li></ul><div class=\"learnbox\"><span class=\"whead\">Learn it:</span><ul><li><b>Concept</b>: <i>Branch protection rule</i> = repo setting that restricts how a branch (here <code>main</code>) changes, e.g. require a PR and approvals. <code>on: pull_request</code> runs a workflow when a PR is opened or updated. <code>on: push</code> runs when commits are pushed. <code>schedule</code> runs on a cron timer. <code>workflow_dispatch</code> is a manual run button.</li><li><b>How it works</b>: Set rules under Settings → Branches (or Rulesets). With \"Require a pull request before merging\" on, direct pushes to <code>main</code> are rejected. Add \"Require status checks\" to make the validation workflow a merge gate. The coding agent only pushes to its own <code>copilot/…</code> branches, so it is not affected.</li><li><b>Example</b>: <code>on:\\n  pull_request:\\n    branches: [main]</code> runs tests on every PR into main.</li><li><b>Exam tip</b>: \"Only via pull requests\" + \"validation when PR opened\" → protection Enabled + <code>pull_request</code>. Trap: <code>push</code> sounds like CI but does not gate PRs.</li></ul></div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/configuring-branches-and-merges-in-your-repository/managing-protected-branches/about-protected-branches\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Branch protection rules</a></li></ul>"},
  {"n":17,"topic":3,"q":9,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You have a repository on github.com that uses the GitHub Copilot coding agent.<br />\nYou also use the GitHub Copilot CLI locally to reproduce failures and continue the same work from your terminal.<br />\nYou need to verify the current token usage.<br />\nWhich Copilot CLI slash command should you run?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\t/compact<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\t/diff<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\t/usage<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\t/context<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<span class=\"whead\">Proposed answers:</span><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"atbl\"><tr><th>Question part</th><th>Answer</th></tr><tr><td>Correct answer</td><td><b>C. /usage</b></td></tr></table></div><span class=\"whead\">Why:</span><ul><li><b>/usage</b>: shows your token / premium request consumption, which is exactly what is asked.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Why not the others:</span><ul><li><b>A. /compact</b>: shrinks the conversation context; does not report tokens.</li><li><b>B. /diff</b>: shows file changes, not tokens.</li><li><b>D. /context</b>: shows what is in the context window, not usage.</li></ul><div class=\"note\"><b>Note:</b> Community votes split between C and D; <code>/context</code> shows context content, while <code>/usage</code> reports usage.</div><div class=\"learnbox\"><span class=\"whead\">Learn it:</span><ul><li><b>Concept</b>: <code>/usage</code> shows token and premium request usage. <code>/context</code> shows what is in the context window. <code>/compact</code> summarizes the conversation to free up context. <code>/diff</code> shows the changes made in the session/working tree.</li><li><b>How it works</b>: Slash commands are typed inside an interactive Copilot CLI session. The context window is limited, so long sessions can be compacted (it also happens automatically near the limit).</li><li><b>Example</b>: After a long debugging chat, run <code>/usage</code> to see consumption, then <code>/compact</code> to shrink the context.</li><li><b>Exam tip</b>: Keyword \"token usage\" → <code>/usage</code>. Trap: <code>/context</code> looks related but shows contents of the window, not consumption.</li></ul></div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/copilot-cli\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot CLI</a></li></ul>"},
  {"n":18,"topic":3,"q":10,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  DRAG DROP<br />\nYour team uses a remote GitHub Model Context Protocol (MCP) server for workflows in the software development life cycle (SDLC).<br />\nYou need to commit a workspace-scoped MCP configuration to ensure that GitHub Copilot can connect to the GitHub-hosted MCP endpoint and authenticate by using a GitHub personal access token (PAT).<br />\nHow should you complete the mcp.json configuration file? To answer, drag the appropriate values to the correct targets. Each value may be used once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.<br />\n<br />\n<img title=\"image22\" src=\"images/image18.png\"><br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<span class=\"whead\">Proposed answers:</span><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"atbl\"><tr><th>#</th><th>Target</th><th>Value</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>\"type\": \" ___ \",</td><td><b>http</b></td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>\"Authorization\": \" ___ ${env:GITHUB_PAT}\"</td><td><b>Bearer</b></td></tr></table></div><span class=\"whead\">Result:</span><pre class=\"acode\">{\n  \"servers\": {\n    \"github\": {\n      \"type\": \"<mark>http</mark>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://api.githubcopilot.com/mcp/\",\n      \"requestInit\": {\n        \"headers\": {\n          \"Authorization\": \"<mark>Bearer</mark> ${env:GITHUB_PAT}\"\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}</pre><span class=\"whead\">Why:</span><ul><li><b>http</b>: the GitHub MCP server is a remote URL, so the transport is HTTP.</li><li><b>Bearer</b>: GitHub PATs are sent as <code>Bearer &lt;token&gt;</code> in the Authorization header.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Why not the others:</span><ul><li><b>Blank 1 – stdio</b>: starts a local process; this server is a remote URL.</li><li><b>Blank 1 – local</b>: not a transport for a hosted URL.</li><li><b>Blank 2 – Digest</b>: a different auth scheme, not used for GitHub PATs.</li><li><b>Blank 2 – Token</b>: not the scheme the GitHub MCP endpoint expects.</li><li><b>Blank 2 – x-api-key</b>: a header name, not a scheme that follows <code>Authorization</code>.</li><li><b>Unused values</b>: <code>http</code> and <code>Bearer</code> each fit only their own blank.</li></ul><div class=\"learnbox\"><span class=\"whead\">Learn it:</span><ul><li><b>Concept</b>: <i>MCP</i> (Model Context Protocol) lets Copilot call external tools. <code>type</code> is the transport: <code>http</code> (remote URL) or <code>stdio</code> (local process Copilot launches). <i>PAT</i> = personal access token. <code>Bearer</code> is the HTTP auth scheme for tokens.</li><li><b>How it works</b>: In VS Code, workspace config lives in <code>.vscode/mcp.json</code>. Remote servers use <code>url</code> plus headers; local servers use <code>command</code> and <code>args</code>. <code>${env:GITHUB_PAT}</code> reads the token from an environment variable so it is not committed.</li><li><b>Example</b>: <code>\"Authorization\": \"Bearer ${env:GITHUB_PAT}\"</code></li><li><b>Exam tip</b>: A <code>url</code> in the config → <code>http</code>. A PAT in a header → <code>Bearer</code>. Digest, Token and x-api-key are distractors.</li></ul></div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/copilot/customization/mcp-servers\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MCP servers in VS Code</a></li></ul>"},
  {"n":19,"topic":3,"q":11,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You have a GitHub Copilot coding agent named CodeAgent. The .agent.md file of CodeAgent contains the following YAML frontmatter.<br />\n---<br />\nname: CodeAgent<br />\ndescription: Performs repository analysis and code review tasks.<br />\ntools: ['edit', 'execute', 'read', 'search']<br />\n---<br />\nYou need to issue a GitHub Copilot CLI command that preserves execution velocity for read-only tasks by eliminating approval prompts for low-risk tools. The solution must ensure that high-risk tools that can make changes remain available but still require explicit user approval before running.<br />\nWhich command should you run?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tcopilot agent run CodeAgent --deny-tool 'edit,execute'<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tcopilot agent run CodeAgent --allow-all-tools<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tcopilot agent run CodeAgent<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tcopilot agent run CodeAgent --allow-tool 'read,search'<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>E.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tcopilot agent run CodeAgent --allow-tool 'read,search' --deny-tool 'edit,execute'<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<span class=\"whead\">Proposed answers:</span><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"atbl\"><tr><th>Question part</th><th>Answer</th></tr><tr><td>Correct answer</td><td><b>D. copilot agent run CodeAgent --allow-tool 'read,search'</b></td></tr></table></div><span class=\"whead\">Why:</span><ul><li><b>--allow-tool</b>: pre-approves read and search, so read-only work runs without prompts.</li><li><b>edit / execute not listed</b>: they stay available but still ask for approval, as required.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Why not the others:</span><ul><li><b>A. copilot agent run CodeAgent --deny-tool 'edit,execute'</b>: reads still prompt, and edit/execute become unavailable.</li><li><b>B. copilot agent run CodeAgent --allow-all-tools</b>: also auto-approves edit and execute.</li><li><b>C. copilot agent run CodeAgent</b>: everything still prompts.</li><li><b>E. copilot agent run CodeAgent --allow-tool 'read,search' --deny-tool 'edit,execute'</b>: denying edit/execute makes them unavailable, but they must remain available.</li></ul><div class=\"learnbox\"><span class=\"whead\">Learn it:</span><ul><li><b>Concept</b>: <code>--allow-tool</code> pre-approves named tools so they run without a prompt. <code>--deny-tool</code> blocks them completely. <code>--allow-all-tools</code> approves everything. The <code>.agent.md</code> <code>tools</code> list says which tools the agent can use.</li><li><b>How it works</b>: In Copilot CLI, tools not pre-approved ask for permission when used. Deny rules take precedence over allow rules.</li><li><b>Example</b>: With <code>--allow-tool 'read,search'</code>, searching files never prompts, but <code>edit</code> still asks first.</li><li><b>Exam tip</b>: \"Low-risk without prompts, high-risk still ask\" → allow only the safe ones. Trap: deny sounds safer but makes tools unavailable, and the question says they must remain available.</li></ul></div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/copilot-cli\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot CLI</a></li></ul>"},
  {"n":20,"topic":3,"q":12,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  DRAG DROP<br />\nYou have a GitHub repository that uses GitHub Actions for CI on pull requests.<br />\nYou have a GitHub Copilot coding agent that opens pull requests for backlog items, and your company requires automated checks for agent-generated changes.<br />\nYou plan to standardize success criteria so that pull requests created by agents only succeed when unit tests pass and CodeQL analysis completes.<br />\nYou need to configure a GitHub Actions workflow that runs on pull requests, executes unit tests, and performs CodeQL analysis.<br />\nHow should you complete the workflow? To answer, drag the appropriate values to the correct targets. Each value may be used once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.<br />\n<br />\n<img title=\"image24\" src=\"images/image20.png\"><br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<span class=\"whead\">Proposed answers:</span><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"atbl\"><tr><th>#</th><th>Target</th><th>Value</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Run tests → <code>run: ___</code></td><td><b>npm test</b></td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Prepare CodeQL → <code>uses: github/codeql-action/ ___ @v3</code></td><td><b>init</b></td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Run CodeQL → <code>uses: github/codeql-action/ ___ @v3</code></td><td><b>analyze</b></td></tr></table></div><span class=\"whead\">Result:</span><pre class=\"acode\">- name: Run tests\n  run: <mark>npm test</mark>\n...\n- name: Prepare CodeQL\n  uses: github/codeql-action/<mark>init</mark>@v3\n- name: Run CodeQL\n  uses: github/codeql-action/<mark>analyze</mark>@v3</pre><span class=\"whead\">Why:</span><ul><li><b>npm test</b>: this is a Node project, so unit tests run with npm.</li><li><b>init</b>: \"Prepare CodeQL\" step: sets up the CodeQL database.</li><li><b>analyze</b>: \"Run CodeQL\" step: runs the queries and reports findings.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Why not the others:</span><ul><li><b>build</b>: compiles code; it does not run unit tests, and is not a CodeQL step.</li><li><b>python -m unittest</b>: Python test runner, wrong for an npm project.</li><li><b>upload-sarif</b>: for results from third-party scanners; CodeQL <code>analyze</code> uploads its own.</li><li><b>Note on order</b>: <code>analyze</code> is not valid in blank 2 and <code>init</code> not in blank 3: CodeQL needs init first, then analyze.</li></ul><div class=\"learnbox\"><span class=\"whead\">Learn it:</span><ul><li><b>Concept</b>: <i>CodeQL</i> is GitHub's code analysis engine for finding security bugs. <code>init</code> sets it up and creates the database; <code>analyze</code> runs the queries and uploads results. <code>autobuild</code>/<code>build</code> compile code. <code>upload-sarif</code> uploads results from other tools (SARIF = result file format).</li><li><b>How it works</b>: Workflow triggers on <code>pull_request</code>. The CodeQL job needs <code>security-events: write</code> to publish alerts. Run <code>init</code> before <code>analyze</code>; for interpreted languages no build is needed. Mark the jobs as required status checks so agent PRs can't merge unless they pass.</li><li><b>Example</b>: <code>uses: github/codeql-action/init@v3</code> then later <code>uses: github/codeql-action/analyze@v3</code>.</li><li><b>Exam tip</b>: Test command must match the stack (<code>npm test</code> for Node). \"Prepare\" → init, \"Run\" → analyze. Trap: upload-sarif is for third-party scanners.</li></ul></div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/code-security/code-scanning/introduction-to-code-scanning/about-code-scanning\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Code scanning / CodeQL</a></li></ul>"},
  {"n":21,"topic":3,"q":13,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You have a GitHub Enterprise Cloud repository that uses the GitHub Copilot coding agent.<br />\nEngineers assign issues to Copilot, and Copilot creates draft pull requests. The engineers start tasks either by assigning issues on github.com or by using GitHub Copilot Chat in an IDE. Reviewers request updates by leaving pull request comments.<br />\nYou discover that sometimes, Copilot uses outdated requirements after a reviewer posts an updated instruction in a pull request comment, and, in several cases, Copilot fails to resume work from the comment.<br />\nYou need to ensure that iteration requests are applied to the correct pull request session and are processed consistently.<br />\nWhat should you do?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tEnsure that the reviewer has write access to the repository. Instruct the reviewer to mention @copilot in the pull request comments.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tEnsure that the agent has write access to the repository. Instruct the reviewer to mention @github in the task comments.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tEnsure that the agent has write access to the repository. Instruct the reviewer to unassign and reassign the original issue to Copilot.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tStart a new task from Copilot Chat in the IDE. Instruct the reviewer to mention @github in the task comments.<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<span class=\"whead\">Proposed answers:</span><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"atbl\"><tr><th>Question part</th><th>Answer</th></tr><tr><td>Correct answer</td><td><b>A. Ensure that the reviewer has write access to the repository. Instruct the reviewer to mention @copilot in the pull request comments.</b></td></tr></table></div><span class=\"whead\">Why:</span><ul><li><b>Write access</b>: Copilot only acts on comments from people who can write to the repo.</li><li><b>@copilot in the PR comment</b>: routes the instruction to the existing session for that PR, with the latest requirements.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Why not the others:</span><ul><li><b>B. Ensure that the agent has write access to the repository. Instruct the reviewer to mention @github in the task comments.</b>: <code>@github</code> is not the agent, and task comments are not the PR session.</li><li><b>C. Ensure that the agent has write access to the repository. Instruct the reviewer to unassign and reassign the original issue to Copilot.</b>: starts a new session and loses context.</li><li><b>D. Start a new task from Copilot Chat in the IDE. Instruct the reviewer to mention @github in the task comments.</b>: a new task is disconnected from the PR session.</li></ul><div class=\"learnbox\"><span class=\"whead\">Learn it:</span><ul><li><b>Concept</b>: The coding agent works from an issue and opens a draft PR. A <i>session</i> is one agent run with its context. <code>@copilot</code> is the mention that addresses the agent. <i>Write access</i> is the permission needed to push to the repo.</li><li><b>How it works</b>: Commenting <code>@copilot</code> on the PR asks the agent to continue that PR's work and push more commits. Only people with write access can trigger it. Comments by others are ignored.</li><li><b>Example</b>: <code>@copilot use the v2 API spec from the latest comment</code> on the PR.</li><li><b>Exam tip</b>: Iterating on a PR → <code>@copilot</code> in the PR comment. Traps: <code>@github</code>, reassigning the issue, or starting a new Chat task.</li></ul></div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/coding-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot coding agent</a></li></ul>"},
  {"n":22,"topic":3,"q":14,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  HOTSPOT<br />\nYour company uses GitHub Copilot custom agents in Microsoft Visual Studio Code.<br />\nThe company also uses the Copilot coding agent on GitHub issues.<br />\nYou have a file named .planner.agent.md that defines an agent named planner.planner has tools set to ['search', 'read', 'fetch']. There are explicit instructions NOT to write or modify any code. The file also defines a handoff labeled Start Implementation to an agent named implementer and sets send to false.<br />\nDevelopers report that after the planner agent produces a plan, implementation sometimes starts immediately in the same conversation, and code changes appear without an explicit agent switch.<br />\nWhen Copilot-created pull requests stall, maintainers review the pull request timeline and session logs. Several stalled sessions show outbound network commands blocked by a firewall, and the repositories do NOT contain a .github/copilot-instructions.md file.<br />\nFor each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.<br />\n<br />\n<img title=\"image26\" src=\"images/image22.png\"><br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<span class=\"whead\">Proposed answers:</span><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"atbl\"><tr><th>#</th><th>Statement</th><th>Answer</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>The configuration of the planner agent explicitly prevents code changes from being produced in the same conversation.</td><td><b>Yes</b></td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>The planner agent is violating its allowed tools by attempting internet access, and removing search will resolve the issue.</td><td><b>No</b></td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Adding a repository-level .github/copilot-instructions.md file that requires the agent to run tests and linters will mitigate the stalled pull request that has a failing CI anti-pattern.</td><td><b>Yes</b></td></tr></table></div><span class=\"whead\">Why:</span><ul><li><b>Statement 1 – Yes</b>: planner has only <code>search</code>, <code>read</code>, <code>fetch</code> (no edit tool) plus an instruction not to write code.</li><li><b>Statement 3 – Yes</b>: a <code>.github/copilot-instructions.md</code> telling the agent to run tests and linters catches failing CI before the PR.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Why not the others:</span><ul><li><b>Statement 2 (answer No)</b>: <code>fetch</code> is in the allowed <code>tools</code> list, so it is not a violation, and the blocked network commands come from the firewall. Removing <code>search</code> fixes neither.</li><li><b>Statements 1 and 3 (answer Yes)</b>: a No here would ignore that <code>planner</code> has no edit tool, and that repo instructions run on every agent task.</li></ul><div class=\"note\"><b>Note:</b> Instructions only steer the agent; the exam still treats them as the mitigation for failing-CI stalls.</div><div class=\"learnbox\"><span class=\"whead\">Learn it:</span><ul><li><b>Concept</b>: A <i>custom agent</i> is defined in <code>.agent.md</code> with YAML frontmatter (<code>name</code>, <code>tools</code>, <code>handoffs</code>). A <i>handoff</i> is a button that moves to another agent; <code>send: false</code> means the prompt is pre-filled but not auto-sent. <code>.github/copilot-instructions.md</code> holds repo-wide instructions. A <i>firewall</i> limits the coding agent's network access.</li><li><b>How it works</b>: The <code>tools</code> list limits what the agent can do; with no edit tool it cannot change files. Repo instructions are added to every agent run, so they can tell it to run tests and linters. Firewall allowlists are configured in the repo's Copilot settings.</li><li><b>Example</b>: <code>tools: ['search','read','fetch']</code> = can research and read, cannot edit.</li><li><b>Exam tip</b>: Judge each statement separately. Trap: assuming tool changes fix firewall problems, which are different things.</li></ul></div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/agent-customization/custom-agents\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Custom agents in VS Code</a></li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/copilot/customization/custom-instructions\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Custom instructions</a></li></ul>"},
  {"n":23,"topic":3,"q":15,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You have a GitHub repository.<br />\nDevelopers use the GitHub Copilot CLI and repository-scoped hooks under .github/hooks/*.json.<br />\nYou need to allow the Copilot CLI to automatically run low-risk Bash commands. The solution must prevent the autonomous execution of high-risk commands, such as sudo, rm -rf /, and curl ... | bash.<br />\nWhat should you do?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tConfigure a userPromptSubmitted hook that logs prompts and exits nonzero.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tAdd .github/hooks/logs/ to .gitignore.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tConfigure a sessionStart hook that prints a policy banner.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tConfigure a preToolUse hook that returns permissionDecision: \"deny\".<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<span class=\"whead\">Proposed answers:</span><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"atbl\"><tr><th>Question part</th><th>Answer</th></tr><tr><td>Correct answer</td><td><b>D. Configure a preToolUse hook that returns permissionDecision: \"deny\".</b></td></tr></table></div><span class=\"whead\">Why:</span><ul><li><b>preToolUse</b>: runs before each tool/Bash command, so it can allow safe ones and deny <code>sudo</code>, <code>rm -rf /</code>, <code>curl | bash</code>.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Why not the others:</span><ul><li><b>A. Configure a userPromptSubmitted hook that logs prompts and exits nonzero.</b>: only sees your prompt, cannot allow or deny individual commands.</li><li><b>B. Add .github/hooks/logs/ to .gitignore.</b>: only hides log files; enforces nothing.</li><li><b>C. Configure a sessionStart hook that prints a policy banner.</b>: prints text once at session start; cannot block.</li></ul><div class=\"learnbox\"><span class=\"whead\">Learn it:</span><ul><li><b>Concept</b>: <i>Hooks</i> are scripts/config under <code>.github/hooks/*.json</code> that run at set points. <code>sessionStart</code> runs at session start. <code>userPromptSubmitted</code> runs when you send a prompt. <code>preToolUse</code> runs before a tool executes. <code>permissionDecision</code> is the result it returns (allow, deny or ask).</li><li><b>How it works</b>: A <code>preToolUse</code> hook receives the tool name and arguments, can inspect a Bash command, and returns a decision. Deny stops the command; allow lets it run.</li><li><b>Example</b>: Deny when the command matches <code>sudo</code>, <code>rm -rf /</code> or <code>curl ... | bash</code>, allow everything else.</li><li><b>Exam tip</b>: \"Block/approve a command before it runs\" → <code>preToolUse</code>. Logging or banner hooks cannot enforce anything.</li></ul></div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/copilot-cli\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot CLI</a></li></ul>"},
  {"n":24,"topic":3,"q":16,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  HOTSPOT<br />\nYour company uses Microsoft Visual Studio Code and GitHub Copilot Chat.<br />\nYou have a GitHub repository that uses main as the default branch. The repository contains a workspace custom agent stored at .github/agents/release-notes.agent.md.<br />\nA developer switches to a branch named branch1 where the agent file does NOT exist. In the same session, the developer switches to a user profile named profile1.profile1 contains a custom agent file named release-notes.agent.md that has user-invokable set to false.<br />\nFor each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.<br />\n<br />\n<img title=\"image28\" src=\"images/image24.png\"><br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<span class=\"whead\">Proposed answers:</span><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"atbl\"><tr><th>#</th><th>Statement</th><th>Answer</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>On main, the agent will appear in the agents dropdown with a display name of release-notes.</td><td><b>Yes</b></td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>When the developer is on branch2, the workspace version of the release-notes agent will be available in the agents dropdown.</td><td><b>No</b></td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>When the developer switches to profile1, the user profile agent will appear in the agents dropdown and will override the workspace agent that has the same file name.</td><td><b>No</b></td></tr></table></div><span class=\"whead\">Why:</span><ul><li><b>Statement 1 – Yes</b>: the file exists in <code>.github/agents/</code> on <code>main</code>, so VS Code lists it.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Why not the others:</span><ul><li><b>Statement 2 (answer No)</b>: that branch has no <code>.github/agents/release-notes.agent.md</code>, so no workspace agent exists.</li><li><b>Statement 3 (answer No)</b>: <code>user-invokable: false</code> hides the profile agent, so it neither appears nor overrides.</li><li><b>Statement 1 (answer Yes)</b>: the file exists on <code>main</code>, so answering No would be wrong.</li></ul><div class=\"note\"><b>Note:</b> The question text says branch1 but the statement says branch2; both lack the file, so the answer is the same.</div><div class=\"learnbox\"><span class=\"whead\">Learn it:</span><ul><li><b>Concept</b>: <i>Workspace agent</i>: <code>.github/agents/*.agent.md</code> in the repo. <i>User profile agent</i>: stored in your VS Code profile, available across workspaces. <code>user-invokable: false</code> hides an agent from the picker (it can still be used by other agents).</li><li><b>How it works</b>: Workspace files exist only on branches that contain them, so switching branches changes the list. Agents are listed by their name/file name.</li><li><b>Example</b>: Checkout <code>main</code> → <code>release-notes</code> appears; checkout a branch without the file → it disappears.</li><li><b>Exam tip</b>: Ask for each statement: is the file on this branch, and is the agent visible? Hidden agents appear nowhere in the dropdown.</li></ul></div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/agent-customization/custom-agents\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Custom agents in VS Code</a></li></ul>"},
  {"n":25,"topic":4,"q":1,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  DRAG DROP<br />\nYou have a GitHub repository that uses a custom GitHub Copilot coding agent defined in the rollout-bot.agent.md file.<br />\nYou need to update a workflow so that agent-profile changes can be rolled back by reverting a single commit and rerunning the workflow. The workflow must check out the exact commit being deployed and apply the agent profile from the repository at that commit.<br />\nHow should you complete the workflow? To answer, drag the appropriate values to the correct targets. Each value may be used once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.<br />\n<br />\n<img title=\"image30\" src=\"images/image26.png\"><br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<span class=\"whead\">Proposed answers:</span><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"atbl\"><tr><th>#</th><th>Target</th><th>Value</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td><code>ref: ${{ ___ }}</code> (Checkout exact ref)</td><td><b>inputs.ref</b></td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td><code>test -f ___</code> (Validate agent profile)</td><td><b>.github/agents/rollout-bot.md</b></td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td><code>./scripts/deploy-agent-profile.sh ___</code></td><td><b>inputs.ref</b></td></tr></table></div><span class=\"whead\">Result:</span><pre class=\"acode\">with:\n  ref: ${{ <mark>inputs.ref</mark> }}\n...\nrun: |\n  test -f <mark>.github/agents/rollout-bot.md</mark>\n...\nrun: |\n  ./scripts/deploy-agent-profile.sh <mark>inputs.ref</mark></pre><span class=\"whead\">Why:</span><ul><li><b>inputs.ref</b>: the ref you choose when running the workflow, so checkout gets that exact commit; reverting and rerunning rolls back.</li><li><b>.github/agents/rollout-bot.md</b>: agent profiles live under <code>.github/agents/</code>.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Why not the others:</span><ul><li><b>${{ github.ref }}</b>: the ref that triggered the run (usually main), not the one you chose.</li><li><b>${{ github.sha }}</b>: the commit that triggered the run, not the chosen one. (Also, <code>${{ }}</code> is already around blank 1.)</li><li><b>github.ref</b>: same problem as above: not the chosen ref.</li><li><b>agents/rollout-bot.md</b>: wrong folder; agent profiles are in <code>.github/agents/</code>.</li></ul><div class=\"note\"><b>Note:</b> Blank 3 follows the old official answer (<code>inputs.ref</code>); the image does not show the answer.</div><div class=\"learnbox\"><span class=\"whead\">Learn it:</span><ul><li><b>Concept</b>: <code>workflow_dispatch</code> is a manual trigger that can take <code>inputs</code>. <code>inputs.ref</code> is the value you typed. <code>github.ref</code> is the ref that triggered the run, <code>github.sha</code> is the commit SHA of that trigger. <code>actions/checkout</code> with <code>ref:</code> checks out a given commit.</li><li><b>How it works</b>: Pinning checkout to an input commit means the run uses exactly that version. To roll back: revert the commit, rerun the workflow with the new commit. Custom agent profiles are stored in <code>.github/agents/</code>.</li><li><b>Example</b>: Run with <code>ref: abc123</code> and the workflow checks out and deploys that commit's profile.</li><li><b>Exam tip</b>: \"Exact commit you choose\" → <code>inputs.ref</code>, not <code>github.ref</code>. Check the folder name too.</li></ul></div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/agent-customization/custom-agents\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Custom agents in VS Code</a></li></ul>"},
  {"n":26,"topic":4,"q":2,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You have a GitHub Enterprise organization that has Copilot memory enabled.<br />\nYou create a new repository.<br />\nWhat are two ways that memories will be deleted from the repository? Each correct answer presents a complete solution.<br><br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tmanually by repository members<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\twhen the repository is archived<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\twhen the code that created the memory is deleted<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tautomatically after 28 days<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>E.\r\n\t\t\t\t\twhen pull requests are merged<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<span class=\"whead\">Proposed answers:</span><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"atbl\"><tr><th>Question part</th><th>Answer</th></tr><tr><td>Correct answers</td><td><b>A. manually by repository members<br>D. automatically after 28 days</b></td></tr></table></div><span class=\"whead\">Why:</span><ul><li><b>A – manual delete</b>: repository members with the right access can delete memories in settings.</li><li><b>D – 28 days</b>: a memory not used for 28 days is removed automatically.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Why not the others:</span><ul><li><b>B. when the repository is archived</b>: archiving deletes nothing.</li><li><b>C. when the code that created the memory is deleted</b>: the memory just stops being validated.</li><li><b>E. when pull requests are merged</b>: merging never deletes memories.</li></ul><div class=\"learnbox\"><span class=\"whead\">Learn it:</span><ul><li><b>Concept</b>: <i>Copilot Memory</i> lets Copilot store useful repository facts it learns (conventions, how things work) and reuse them later. Memories are tied to the repository.</li><li><b>How it works</b>: Each stored memory is checked against the current code before use. Memories expire automatically after 28 days, and repository admins or owners can review and delete them in repository settings.</li><li><b>Example</b>: A memory like \"use <code>pnpm</code> to install\" disappears after 28 days unless it is refreshed, or when an admin deletes it.</li><li><b>Exam tip</b>: Remember the two ways: manual delete and 28 days. Archiving, deleting code or merging PRs are distractors.</li></ul></div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/copilot-memory\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot Memory (concepts)</a></li></ul>"},
  {"n":27,"topic":4,"q":3,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  HOTSPOT<br />\nYou have a GitHub Enterprise organization that uses GitHub Copilot.<br />\nYou discover that GitHub Copilot Chat responses in Microsoft Visual Studio Code are influenced by earlier, unrelated troubleshooting prompts from the same conversation.<br />\nYou need to ensure that the Copilot Chat conversation context is limited to information relevant to the current work item. The solution must minimize effort.<br />\nWhat should you do? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.<br />\n<br />\n<img title=\"image32\" src=\"images/image28.png\"><br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<span class=\"whead\">Proposed answers:</span><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"atbl\"><tr><th>#</th><th>Target</th><th>Value</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>When a previous prompt is no longer relevant:</td><td><b>Start a new chat.</b></td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>To keep Copilot Chat focused in the IDE:</td><td><b>Open related files and close unrelated files.</b></td></tr></table></div><span class=\"whead\">Why:</span><ul><li><b>Start a new chat</b>: clears old history in one click, the least effort.</li><li><b>Open related / close unrelated files</b>: open files are context, so this steers answers to the current work.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Why not the others:</span><ul><li><b>Dropdown 1 – Delete the repository memories.</b>: removes useful repo knowledge; the problem is chat history.</li><li><b>Dropdown 1 – Delete the irrelevant request from the conversation.</b>: fiddly, leaves other old context.</li><li><b>Dropdown 2 – Change from chat mode to agent mode.</b>: changes behavior, not context.</li><li><b>Dropdown 2 – Disable all Copilot features for the repository.</b>: overkill for a context problem.</li></ul><div class=\"learnbox\"><span class=\"whead\">Learn it:</span><ul><li><b>Concept</b>: <i>Context</i> is what Copilot Chat reads when answering: the conversation history, open and attached files, and selected code. A <i>chat session</i> is one conversation with its own history. <i>Memories</i> are saved repo facts. <i>Agent mode</i> lets Copilot make edits and run tools.</li><li><b>How it works</b>: Everything earlier in the same chat can influence replies. A new chat starts empty. Files you open or attach add relevant context.</li><li><b>Example</b>: After debugging a bug, click <i>New chat</i> before asking about docs, and keep only the docs files open.</li><li><b>Exam tip</b>: \"Old unrelated prompts influence answers\" → new chat. \"Stay focused\" → relevant files open. Disabling features is never the minimal-effort answer.</li></ul></div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/coding-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot coding agent</a></li></ul>"},
  {"n":28,"topic":4,"q":4,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  DRAG DROP<br />\nYour company uses GitHub Copilot Enterprise.<br />\nDevelopers use GitHub Copilot agent mode in Microsoft Visual Studio Code on their laptops and Copilot Chat on github.com when they are away from their laptops.<br />\nWhen switching between environments, the developers notice that agent workflows lose continuity because the tools available in Visual Studio Code are unavailable on github.com.<br />\nYou need to ensure that the agent tools and state are available consistently across environments and can be used from any device without local setup.<br />\nWhat should you do for each requirement? To answer, drag the appropriate actions to the correct requirements. Each action may be used once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.<br />\n<br />\n<img title=\"image34\" src=\"images/image30.png\"><br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<span class=\"whead\">Proposed answers:</span><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"atbl\"><tr><th>#</th><th>Target</th><th>Value</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>To allow cross-device tool access without local setup:</td><td><b>Use a GitHub-hosted Model Context Protocol (MCP) server for cloud-based workflows.</b></td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>To enable organization-wide agent tools across all Copilot environments:</td><td><b>Enable the MCP servers in Copilot policy.</b></td></tr></table></div><span class=\"whead\">Why:</span><ul><li><b>GitHub-hosted MCP server</b>: lives in the cloud, so the same tools work in VS Code and on github.com with nothing to install.</li><li><b>Copilot policy</b>: one org setting turns MCP on for every Copilot environment.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Why not the others:</span><ul><li><b>Run a Model Context Protocol (MCP) server in Docker.</b>: local to one machine, not on github.com.</li><li><b>Add third-party Model Context Protocol (MCP) servers by using the /mcp add command.</b>: manual per-device setup.</li><li><b>From Visual Studio Code, change the Copilot mode.</b>: does not change which tools exist.</li><li><b>Configure toolsets to disable unused GitHub API groups.</b>: removes tools instead of sharing them.</li></ul><div class=\"learnbox\"><span class=\"whead\">Learn it:</span><ul><li><b>Concept</b>: <i>MCP server</i> = a service giving Copilot extra tools. <i>GitHub-hosted</i> MCP server runs in GitHub's cloud. Local ones run on your machine (e.g. in Docker). <i>Toolsets</i> group GitHub API tools to enable or disable. <i>Copilot policy</i> = org-level settings that allow features.</li><li><b>How it works</b>: A hosted server is reachable from any device with your account, so tools are the same everywhere. An organization owner enables the <i>MCP servers in Copilot</i> policy in Copilot settings, which allows MCP use across environments.</li><li><b>Example</b>: Use the GitHub MCP server at <code>https://api.githubcopilot.com/mcp/</code> from VS Code and elsewhere without installing anything.</li><li><b>Exam tip</b>: \"No local setup / any device\" → hosted. \"Organization-wide\" → policy. Local Docker and <code>/mcp add</code> are per-machine.</li></ul></div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/copilot/customization/mcp-servers\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MCP servers in VS Code</a></li></ul>"},
  {"n":29,"topic":4,"q":5,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  HOTSPOT<br />\nYou have a GitHub repository that uses the GitHub Copilot coding agent to resolve issues and create draft pull requests.<br />\nYou assign an issue to Copilot. Copilot creates a draft pull request. The pull request timeline shows Copilot started work, followed by status updates. The most recent status update is 55 minutes old.<br />\nYou discover that the agent is no longer making progress.<br />\nYou need to ensure that the work resumes without redoing the completed steps or changing the previously chosen approach.<br />\nWhat should you do? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.<br />\n<br />\n<img title=\"image36\" src=\"images/image32.png\"><br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<span class=\"whead\">Proposed answers:</span><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"atbl\"><tr><th>#</th><th>Target</th><th>Value</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>To confirm progress of the agent:</td><td><b>Select View session from the pull request.</b></td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>To resume the work:</td><td><b>Post a pull request comment that mentions @copilot.</b></td></tr></table></div><span class=\"whead\">Why:</span><ul><li><b>View session</b>: shows what the agent is doing right now, to confirm it is stuck.</li><li><b>@copilot comment on the PR</b>: nudges the same session, so finished steps and the chosen approach are kept.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Why not the others:</span><ul><li><b>Review insights for the repository.</b>: shows repo activity, not agent progress.</li><li><b>Run git log locally on the default branch.</b>: shows commits, not the agent session.</li><li><b>Close and reopen the issue.</b>: may restart work and redo steps.</li><li><b>Mark the draft pull request as Ready for review.</b>: the work is not finished.</li><li><b>From GitHub Copilot Chat, open a new pull request.</b>: starts a new session and approach.</li></ul><div class=\"learnbox\"><span class=\"whead\">Learn it:</span><ul><li><b>Concept</b>: <i>View session</i> opens the agent's session log, showing steps it has taken. <i>Draft PR</i> is a PR not yet ready for review. <code>@copilot</code> comment tells the agent to act on the PR.</li><li><b>How it works</b>: The session log shows whether the agent is running, waiting or failed. A PR comment mentioning <code>@copilot</code> continues the same session on the same branch.</li><li><b>Example</b>: Open the PR → <i>View session</i> → see where it stopped → comment <code>@copilot please continue</code>.</li><li><b>Exam tip</b>: Pair \"check progress\" with the session view and \"resume\" with an <code>@copilot</code> PR comment. Closing, reopening or new PRs lose the earlier work.</li></ul></div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/coding-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot coding agent</a></li></ul>"},
  {"n":30,"topic":4,"q":6,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You have a GitHub repository that uses the GitHub Copilot coding agent.<br />\nYour company restricts GitHub Actions secrets.<br />\nDevelopers need the Copilot coding agent to call an internal dependency-scanning API during its run. The API requires an access token.<br />\nYou need to ensure that the Copilot coding agent can use the token during execution without accessing the repository's Actions secrets and variables. The solution must prevent exposing the token in plaintext.<br />\nWhat should you do?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tAdd the token as an Actions repository secret.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tAdd the token as a secret in the Copilot environment.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tStore the token in a repository custom instructions file.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tStore the token in the agent configuration file.<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<span class=\"whead\">Proposed answers:</span><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"atbl\"><tr><th>Question part</th><th>Answer</th></tr><tr><td>Correct answer</td><td><b>B. Add the token as a secret in the Copilot environment.</b></td></tr></table></div><span class=\"whead\">Why:</span><ul><li><b>Copilot environment secret</b>: the agent reads secrets from here during its run, without touching Actions secrets, and it is not shown in plaintext.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Why not the others:</span><ul><li><b>A. Add the token as an Actions repository secret.</b>: the agent does not use Actions secrets, which the question rules out.</li><li><b>C. Store the token in a repository custom instructions file.</b>: committed file, token in plaintext.</li><li><b>D. Store the token in the agent configuration file.</b>: committed file, token in plaintext.</li></ul><div class=\"learnbox\"><span class=\"whead\">Learn it:</span><ul><li><b>Concept</b>: The <i>Copilot environment</i> is the agent's own setup (under Settings → Environments → <code>copilot</code>), with its own secrets and variables. <i>Actions secrets</i> are for workflows. Files in the repo (instructions, configs) are plaintext.</li><li><b>How it works</b>: Secrets added to the <code>copilot</code> environment are available to the coding agent's session. The agent does not get repository Actions secrets and variables.</li><li><b>Example</b>: Add <code>SCAN_API_TOKEN</code> as a <code>copilot</code> environment secret so the agent can call the scanning API.</li><li><b>Exam tip</b>: \"Agent needs a secret\" → Copilot environment secret. Never commit tokens to files.</li></ul></div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/coding-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot coding agent</a></li></ul>"},
  {"n":31,"topic":5,"q":1,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You have a private GitHub repository that has Copilot memory enabled.<br />\nSeveral developers who have write access to the repository make changes across multiple branches, including creating some pull requests that are later closed without merging.<br />\nYour team needs to understand how GitHub Copilot ensures that only task-relevant, up-to-date information influences code suggestions, even when older memories exist.<br />\nHow does Copilot manage memories?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tCopilot validates each memory's citations against the current branch before using the memory, and ignores the memory if the referenced code no longer exists.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tCopilot stores memories per user, ensuring that only the developer who created a memory can trigger the memory in future sessions.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tCopilot stores memories indefinitely until a repository administrator deletes them manually.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tCopilot automatically blocks memory creation from pull requests that are closed without merging, to prevent outdated information from being stored.<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<span class=\"whead\">Proposed answers:</span><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"atbl\"><tr><th>Question part</th><th>Answer</th></tr><tr><td>Correct answer</td><td><b>A – Copilot validates each memory's citations against the current branch before using the memory, and ignores the memory if the referenced code no longer exists.</b></td></tr></table></div><span class=\"whead\">Why:</span><ul><li><b>Citations</b>: each memory points at code that supports it.</li><li><b>Check before use</b>: Copilot re-checks that code on the current branch each time.</li><li><b>Stale = skipped</b>: if the code moved or was deleted, the memory is ignored, so old info cannot steer suggestions.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Why not the others:</span><ul><li><b>B – “Copilot stores memories per user, ensuring that only the developer who created a memory can trigger the memory in future sessions.”</b>: repository memories are shared, not locked to the developer who created them.</li><li><b>C – “Copilot stores memories indefinitely until a repository administrator deletes them manually.”</b>: memories are not kept forever; unused ones expire after 28 days.</li><li><b>D – “Copilot automatically blocks memory creation from pull requests that are closed without merging, to prevent outdated information from being stored.”</b>: closed-unmerged PRs can still seed memories; they are not blocked.</li></ul><div class=\"learnbox\"><span class=\"whead\">Learn it:</span><ul><li><b>Concept</b>: <b>Copilot Memory</b> lets Copilot save useful facts about a repository (for example \"tests use pytest\") and reuse them later. A <b>citation</b> is a pointer to the code that supports a memory. A <b>stale</b> memory is one whose supporting code has changed or disappeared.</li><li><b>How it works</b>: Before using a memory, Copilot checks its citations against the branch you are working on. If the code is gone, the memory is not used. Memories that go unused expire automatically after 28 days. Memories are shared at repository level, not private per user.</li><li><b>Example</b>: A memory says \"retry logic lives in <code>net/retry.go</code>\". If a refactor deletes that file, Copilot skips the memory; if the file still exists, the memory is used.</li><li><b>Exam tip</b>: Words like \"up-to-date\", \"older memories\" and \"task-relevant\" point to citation validation. Trap: thinking memories are per user, manual-delete only, or never created from closed PRs.</li></ul></div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/copilot-memory\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot Memory (concepts)</a></li></ul>"},
  {"n":32,"topic":5,"q":2,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  Case study<br><br>Existing Environment<br />\nGitHub Environment<br />\nThe GitHub environment contains the following:<br />\nThree repositories named product-api, billing-service, and infra-terraform.<br />\nBranch protection on the main branch in all repositories that requires at least one pull request review before merging<br />\nGitHub Actions runners used across all workflows<br />\nA GitHub team named SG_Dev that contains developers<br />\nA GitHub team named SG_Review that contains senior engineers and a security team<br />\nA .github/copilot-instructions.md file that includes general coding conventions for all features<br />\nAgent environment<br />\nThe product-api repository uses a GitHub Copilot coding agent named agent1 that has the following configurations:<br />\nNo custom agent profile is defined.<br />\nA Model Context Protocol (MCP) server named MCP1 is deployed to https://mcp.litwareinc.internal and provides access to internal ticketing and deployment APIs. MCP1 requires an API key for authentication.<br />\nA second Copilot coding agent named agent2 handles changes in infra-terraform and runs in parallel with agent1 when both agents have open assigned issues.<br />\nCopilot memory is NOT enabled for the organization.<br />\nProblem Statements<br />\nLitware identifies the following issues:<br />\nDuring two recent sessions, agent1 accessed files in billing-service, which is outside the agent's intended scope.<br />\nagent1 makes code changes immediately after receiving a task.<br />\nA developer named Ben, who is on the SG_Dev team, reports that agent1 completed a session with a successful status and opened a pull request, but the pull request contains no file changes. Other developers report this intermittently as well.<br />\nBoth agent1 and agent2 modified shared/config.yaml in a parallel test run, generating conflicting outputs.<br />\nagent1 consistently uses raw try-catch blocks for error handling, which violates the defined implementation guidelines of SG_Dev.<br />\nRequirements<br />\nPlanned Changes<br />\nLitware plans to make the following changes:<br />\nEnsure that agent1 can access all the tools in the environment.<br />\nProvide product-api with specific instructions to agent1 without affecting Copilot Chat or Copilot code review.<br />\nConfigure MCP1 as a tool for agent1 by modifying the product-api repository MCP configuration.<br />\nEnsure that Copilot retains details that it has learned and uses that knowledge for future work. This must be applied to all licensed members of the organization.<br />\nImplementation guidelines<br />\nThe development team at Litware identifies the following implementation guidelines:<br />\nAgent workflows must be able to run in parallel.<br />\nApplication error handling must use the repository ErrorHandler class.<br />\nagent1 and agent2 must run on isolated branches during parallel execution. File-level conflicts must be detected before merges, and both agents must be able to run concurrently.<br />\nSecurity requirements<br />\nLitware identifies the following security requirements:<br />\nOnly the members of SG_Review must be able to approve agent1 plan outputs.<br />\nAll API keys must be stored and accessed securely.<br />\nThe developers must NOT be able to self-approve.<br />\nAgent configuration<br />\n<img title=\"image38\" src=\"images/image10.png\"><br />\nYou need to troubleshoot the issue reported by Ben.<br />\nWhat should you review?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tthe GITHUB_TOKEN permissions block in the agent1 workflow<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tthe agent session log in the Agents panel<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tthe GitHub Actions runner log for the session job<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tthe GitHub Actions usage metrics of repo1<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<span class=\"whead\">Proposed answers:</span><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"atbl\"><tr><th>Question part</th><th>Answer</th></tr><tr><td>Correct answer</td><td><b>B – the agent session log in the Agents panel</b></td></tr></table></div><span class=\"whead\">Why:</span><ul><li><b>Session log</b>: records what the agent read, decided and why it made no changes.</li><li><b>Ben's case</b>: the session succeeded but the PR is empty, so you need the agent's reasoning, not infrastructure output.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Why not the others:</span><ul><li><b>A – “the GITHUB_TOKEN permissions block in the agent1 workflow”</b>: show what the agent may access, not what it decided.</li><li><b>C – “the GitHub Actions runner log for the session job”</b>: shows setup and command output; it is green even if the agent chose to change nothing.</li><li><b>D – “the GitHub Actions usage metrics of repo1”</b>: only aggregate numbers across sessions.</li></ul><div class=\"learnbox\"><span class=\"whead\">Learn it:</span><ul><li><b>Concept</b>: The <b>agent session log</b> (Agents panel) is the step-by-step record of what the coding agent read, thought and did. The <b>Actions runner log</b> is the output of the machine that ran the job. <code>GITHUB_TOKEN</code> permissions are the access rights of the workflow. <b>Usage metrics</b> are summary numbers.</li><li><b>How it works</b>: The agent works in an Actions-based environment and opens a PR; the session is shown with a log you can open from the PR or the Agents panel. The log includes its reasoning, files explored and tools called, so you can see why no file was changed.</li><li><b>Example</b>: Log line: \"SSO flag already handled in middleware, no change needed\". That explains a successful session with an empty PR.</li><li><b>Exam tip</b>: \"Why did the agent do (or not do) X\" means the session log. Trap: picking runner logs, which only show infrastructure steps.</li></ul></div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/coding-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot coding agent</a></li></ul>"},
  {"n":33,"topic":5,"q":3,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  HOTSPOT<br><br>Overview<br />\nContoso Ltd. is a software development company located in the United States.<br />\nExisting Environment<br />\nGitHub Environment<br />\nContoso uses GitHub Enterprise and assigns GitHub Copilot Pro+ licenses to its developers. The developers use Microsoft Visual Studio Code as their IDE.<br />\nContoso has a customer portal. The code for the portal is stored in a GitHub repository named repo1 that contains the following:<br />\nA custom agent named agent1 that includes instructions to review specs related to best practices<br />\nA custom instruction file named validate-instructions.md that is used to validate tone of voice and applies to all .md and .txt files<br />\nA custom instruction file named codereview.instructions.md that is used by the Copilot coding agent but is excluded for use by the Copilot code review<br />\nrepo1 has the following structure:<br />\nThe front-end is stored in the /frontend folder.<br />\nThe API logic is stored in the /api folder.<br />\nContoso has a second repository named repo2 that contains a legacy .NET application named App1 built by using .NET 6. repo2 has a multi-agent workflow for modernization tasks.<br />\nContoso enables the Model Context Protocol (MCP) registry and allows the Microsoft Learn MCP Server. Every developer must configure their own connection to the Learn MCP Server.<br />\nProblem Statements<br />\nThe developers working in repo1 report that the Microsoft Learn documentation is NOT being retrieved when they attempt to validate a design by using agent1.<br />\nThe testing team at Contoso identifies that the customer portal uses inconsistent UI styles, which leads to customer confusion and branding issues. The UI inconsistencies stem from variations in the folder structure.<br />\nAgent Logs<br />\nYou have the following logs for the multi-agent workflow used in repo2.<br />\n<img title=\"image39\" src=\"images/image1.png\"><br />\nRequirements<br />\nPlanned Changes<br />\nContoso plans to have all agents and developers in repo1 use the Microsoft Learn MCP to ensure that reviews are validated by using the appropriate documentation. This must be implemented centrally.<br />\nContoso plans to leverage AI-powered coding agents to implement new portal features and pages.<br />\nTechnical Requirements<br />\nApp1 must be upgraded to .NET 10. A previous upgrade attempt was started by using the Copilot modernization agent, but the attempt was never finalized.<br />\nYou plan to retry the upgrade. You must first analyze App1 by using AI, and then generate a report that contains breaking changes and deprecated patterns before retrying the upgrade.<br />\nAll AI-generated code for UI styling must adhere to a predefined folder structure.<br />\nThe architects at Contoso need help building implementation plans for repo1. The company wants to implement a new agent named agent2 to analyze the code base and the code requirements, and then respond with a detailed plan. The agent must NOT be able to edit files or run local commands.<br />\nThe developers must be able to delegate work to the Copilot coding agent by assigning issues to the agent.<br />\nYou are evaluating the logs of the multi-agent workflow in repo2.<br />\nFor each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.<br />\n<br />\n<img title=\"image40\" src=\"images/image34.png\"><br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<span class=\"whead\">Proposed answers:</span><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"atbl\"><tr><th>#</th><th>Statement (as shown in the question)</th><th>Answer</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>The Github MCP server is enabled for the agent sessions.</td><td><b>Yes</b></td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Workspace isolation is being used by the agent sessions.</td><td><b>No</b></td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>The logs show that exactly two agent sessions have started.</td><td><b>Yes</b></td></tr></table></div><span class=\"whead\">Result:</span><pre class=\"acode\"><mark>Passing 1 MCP server(s) to SDK: [github]</mark>\n<mark>isolationEnabled: false</mark>\n<mark>Invoking ... isNewSession: true  (de04f0fe)</mark>\n<mark>Invoking ... isNewSession: true  (6852c5dc)</pre></mark><span class=\"whead\">Why:</span><ul><li><b>MCP server – Yes</b>: <code>Passing 1 MCP server(s) ... [github]</code> shows the GitHub server was handed to the session.</li><li><b>Isolation – No</b>: <code>isolationEnabled: false</code> and no worktree path, so isolation never ran.</li><li><b>Two sessions – Yes</b>: two different <code>isNewSession: true</code> IDs (<code>de04f0fe</code>, <code>6852c5dc</code>).</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Why not the others:</span><ul><li><b><code>CLIMCPServerEnabled=false</code></b>: only controls extra CLI-side servers; it does not mean the GitHub server is off.</li><li><b>Request IDs (<code>ccreq</code>)</b>: these are per call, not per session, so they are not counted as sessions.</li></ul><div class=\"learnbox\"><span class=\"whead\">Learn it:</span><ul><li><b>Concept</b>: <b>MCP server</b> = a tool provider the agent can call (GitHub, docs, tickets). <b>Isolation / worktree</b> = running each agent in its own separate copy of the code so parallel agents do not collide. <b>Session</b> = one agent run, identified by a unique ID.</li><li><b>How it works</b>: Read logs literally. <code>Passing N MCP server(s) to SDK: [name]</code> lists the servers the session received. <code>isolationEnabled: true</code> plus a worktree path means isolation is active. Each new session logs <code>isNewSession: true</code> with its own ID.</li><li><b>Example</b>: Two lines with <code>isNewSession: true</code> and IDs <code>de04f0fe</code> and <code>6852c5dc</code> = exactly two sessions, even if there are many request lines.</li><li><b>Exam tip</b>: Log-reading Yes/No questions: count and quote exact fields, ignore guesses. Trap: <code>CLIMCPServerEnabled=false</code> does not disable the GitHub MCP server, and request IDs are not session IDs.</li></ul></div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/copilot-cli\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot CLI</a></li><li><a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/copilot/customization/mcp-servers\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MCP servers in VS Code</a></li></ul>"},
  {"n":34,"topic":5,"q":4,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  HOTSPOT<br />\nYou have the following agent logs.<br />\n<img title=\"image42\" src=\"images/image36.png\"><br />\nFor each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.<br />\n<br />\n<img title=\"image43\" src=\"images/image37.png\"><br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<span class=\"whead\">Proposed answers:</span><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"atbl\"><tr><th>#</th><th>Statement (as shown in the question)</th><th>Answer</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>[Statement_2] The agent responded with messages.</td><td><b>No</b></td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>[Statement_3] The agent can edit files in the repository.</td><td><b>No</b></td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>[Statement_1] The agent analyzed files in the repository.</td><td><b>No</b></td></tr></table></div><span class=\"whead\">Result:</span><pre class=\"acode\">... [ToolCallingLoop] ... <mark>message 0 returned</mark>. finish reason: [stop]\n... gpt-5.3-codex | <mark>[panel/editAgent]</mark></pre><span class=\"whead\">Why:</span><ul><li><b>Responded – No</b>: <code>message 0 returned</code> means the model produced zero messages.</li><li><b>Edit files – No</b>: <code>[panel/editAgent]</code> is just the VS Code panel the call came from; no file edit appears.</li><li><b>Analyzed files – No</b>: no log line shows a file being read, searched or analyzed.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Why not \"Yes\":</span><ul><li><b>Model calls ≠ work</b>: lines like <code>success | gpt-5.3-codex</code> only show the model was called.</li><li><b>Names ≠ actions</b>: a panel name or <code>copilotmd</code> label describes plumbing, not an operation on the repo.</li></ul><div class=\"note\"><b>Note:</b> The statement labels (<code>[Statement_2]</code>, <code>[Statement_3]</code>, <code>[Statement_1]</code>) appear in this shuffled order in the question; the table keeps that order. All three answers are No.</div><div class=\"learnbox\"><span class=\"whead\">Learn it:</span><ul><li><b>Concept</b>: An <b>agent log</b> mixes model calls, UI tags and real actions. <b>Messages</b> are the replies the agent produces. A <b>file operation</b> (read, search, edit) always names a tool and usually a path.</li><li><b>How it works</b>: <code>message 0 returned</code> with finish reason <code>stop</code> means the call ended without producing any message. Tags such as <code>[panel/editAgent]</code> only say which VS Code panel started the call. Model names (<code>gpt-5.3-codex</code>) and <code>success | 10385ms</code> only describe the model request.</li><li><b>Example</b>: A real file analysis would look like \"read file <code>src/app.js</code>\" or \"grep for ...\". Nothing like that is in this log.</li><li><b>Exam tip</b>: Only verbs with targets (read, edit, search plus a path) prove work. Trap: reading a label such as \"editAgent\" as evidence that files were edited.</li></ul></div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/coding-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot coding agent</a></li></ul>"},
  {"n":35,"topic":5,"q":5,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You have a GitHub repository that uses GitHub Copilot code review on pull requests.<br />\nYou plan to add repository-wide code review guidance that will apply to all files.<br />\nYou need Copilot code review to consistently apply the guidance during pull request reviews.<br />\nWhat should you do?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tModify the .github/pull_request_template.md file.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tUpdate the instructions in the .github/agents/*.agent.md files.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tCreate a .github/instructions/copilot.instructions.md file.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tCreate a .github/copilot-instructions.md file.<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<span class=\"whead\">Proposed answers:</span><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"atbl\"><tr><th>Question part</th><th>Answer</th></tr><tr><td>Correct answer</td><td><b>D – Create a .github/copilot-instructions.md file.</b></td></tr></table></div><span class=\"whead\">Why:</span><ul><li><b>Repo-wide file</b>: <code>.github/copilot-instructions.md</code> applies to the whole repository.</li><li><b>Code review reads it</b>: Copilot code review uses it on every pull request, for all files.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Why not the others:</span><ul><li><b>A – “Modify the .github/pull_request_template.md file.”</b>: pre-fills the PR text for people; Copilot review does not treat it as rules.</li><li><b>B – “Update the instructions in the .github/agents/*.agent.md files.”</b>: configures one custom agent, not code review.</li><li><b>C – “Create a .github/instructions/copilot.instructions.md file.”</b>: path-scoped files need <code>applyTo</code> and only match some files; it is not the repo-wide file.</li></ul><div class=\"learnbox\"><span class=\"whead\">Learn it:</span><ul><li><b>Concept</b>: <b><code>.github/copilot-instructions.md</code></b> = repo-wide instructions. <b><code>.github/instructions/*.instructions.md</code></b> = instructions limited to files matching an <code>applyTo</code> glob. <b><code>.github/agents/*.agent.md</code></b> = definition of a custom agent. <b>PR template</b> = text pre-filled in new pull requests.</li><li><b>How it works</b>: Copilot code review reads the repo-wide file and matching path-specific files when reviewing a pull request. Repo-wide rules go in the single file; path-specific ones add <code>applyTo: \"**/*.ts\"</code> in the front matter. Code review reads only the first part of each instruction file (about 4,000 characters).</li><li><b>Example</b>: <code>.github/copilot-instructions.md</code>: \"Flag any PR that adds code without tests.\"</li><li><b>Exam tip</b>: \"Repository-wide\" and \"all files\" point to <code>copilot-instructions.md</code>. Trap: a path-specific file named <code>copilot.instructions.md</code> sounds right but is not the repo-wide file.</li></ul></div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/copilot/customization/custom-instructions\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Custom instructions</a></li></ul>"},
  {"n":36,"topic":5,"q":6,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You have a GitHub Enterprise repository that uses the GitHub Copilot coding agent and opens draft pull requests for assigned issues.<br />\nEvaluation results show that the agent repeatedly opens pull requests that modify .github/workflows/*.yml to bypass failing checks instead of fixing the underlying code.<br />\nYou need to ensure that the agent fixes the underlying code instead of bypassing the failing checks.<br />\nWhat should you do?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tStart the GitHub Copilot CLI by using --allow-all.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tUpdate .github/copilot-instructions.md.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tCreate a branch ruleset.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tDisable GitHub Actions workflows for pull requests created by the agent.<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<span class=\"whead\">Proposed answers:</span><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"atbl\"><tr><th>Question part</th><th>Answer</th></tr><tr><td>Correct answer</td><td><b>B – Update .github/copilot-instructions.md.</b></td></tr></table></div><span class=\"whead\">Why:</span><ul><li><b>Teach the goal</b>: the agent bypasses checks because nothing tells it to fix the code instead.</li><li><b>Instructions</b>: add rules such as \"never edit workflows to pass checks; fix the underlying code\".</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Why not the others:</span><ul><li><b>A – “Start the GitHub Copilot CLI by using --allow-all.”</b>: gives the agent more freedom, which makes it worse.</li><li><b>C – “Create a branch ruleset.”</b>: blocks the workflow edit but does not tell the agent how to fix the real problem; it just gets stuck.</li><li><b>D – “Disable GitHub Actions workflows for pull requests created by the agent.”</b>: removes the checks entirely, so nothing validates the code.</li></ul><div class=\"learnbox\"><span class=\"whead\">Learn it:</span><ul><li><b>Concept</b>: <b>Copilot instructions</b> tell the agent how to work and what the goal is. A <b>branch ruleset</b> enforces rules on branches (who can push, required checks). <code>--allow-all</code> is a Copilot CLI flag that grants all tool permissions without asking.</li><li><b>How it works</b>: The coding agent reads <code>.github/copilot-instructions.md</code> at the start of each task. Rules written there shape its strategy, such as \"fix the code, never change CI to make checks pass\". A ruleset only blocks actions; it does not teach the agent what to do instead.</li><li><b>Example</b>: Add: \"Never edit <code>.github/workflows/*</code> to pass checks. If a check fails, fix the source code and re-run.\"</li><li><b>Exam tip</b>: When the agent behaves badly because it lacks guidance, the answer is instructions. Trap: guardrail options (rulesets, disabling Actions) that stop the symptom but break the workflow, and anything that adds more permissions.</li></ul></div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/copilot/customization/custom-instructions\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Custom instructions</a></li></ul>"},
  {"n":37,"topic":5,"q":7,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You use a GitHub Actions workflow to orchestrate a multi-agent draft-review process for pull requests. The workflow uploads a single combined review-package artifact at the end of each run.<br />\nYou discover that the review-package artifact is occasionally missing from the workflow run history, and you suspect that a user deleted it.<br />\nYou need to identify which user manually deleted a workflow run artifact and when the deletion occurred.<br />\nWhat should you use?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tthe checks.delete_logs audit log event<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tthe pull request description and comment history<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tthe merge commit history<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tthe artifact.destroy audit log event<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<span class=\"whead\">Proposed answers:</span><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"atbl\"><tr><th>Question part</th><th>Answer</th></tr><tr><td>Correct answer</td><td><b>D – the artifact.destroy audit log event</b></td></tr></table></div><span class=\"whead\">Why:</span><ul><li><b>Audit log</b>: records the actor and time for security-relevant actions.</li><li><b><code>artifact.destroy</code></b>: is the event written when someone deletes a workflow artifact, so it answers who and when.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Why not the others:</span><ul><li><b>A – “the checks.delete_logs audit log event”</b>: is about deleting check-run logs, not artifacts.</li><li><b>B – “the pull request description and comment history”</b>: only hold discussion text.</li><li><b>C – “the merge commit history”</b>: shows code merges, not artifact deletions.</li></ul><div class=\"learnbox\"><span class=\"whead\">Learn it:</span><ul><li><b>Concept</b>: The <b>audit log</b> records who did what and when in an organization or enterprise. Each entry has an <b>action</b> name such as <code>artifact.destroy</code> (an artifact was deleted) or <code>checks.delete_logs</code> (check logs were deleted). A <b>workflow artifact</b> is a file saved from a workflow run.</li><li><b>How it works</b>: In the audit log, search with a filter like <code>action:artifact.destroy</code>. Each result shows the actor, repository and timestamp. Audit log events can also be exported or streamed.</li><li><b>Example</b>: <code>action:artifact.destroy repo:contoso/portal</code> returns \"jsmith deleted review-package at 14:03\".</li><li><b>Exam tip</b>: \"Who deleted X and when\" means audit log. Match the event name to the object: artifact means <code>artifact.destroy</code>. Trap: <code>checks.delete_logs</code> is about check run logs.</li></ul></div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/enterprise-cloud@latest/admin/monitoring-activity-in-your-enterprise/reviewing-audit-logs-for-your-enterprise/streaming-the-audit-log-for-your-enterprise\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Audit log streaming</a></li></ul>"},
  {"n":38,"topic":5,"q":8,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You have a GitHub Enterprise Cloud Organization that uses the GitHub Copilot coding agent.<br />\nCopilot creates a draft pull request for an assigned issue, and the pull request timeline shows Copilot started work.<br />\nAfter 70 minutes, the agent session log stops updating, and the pull request body status stops changing.<br />\nYou need to restart the agent so that it continues the task from the issue context and produces new commits to the existing draft pull request.<br />\nWhat should you do?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tWait for the agent to complete.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tMerge the draft pull request and mention @copilot on the merged pull request.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tUnassign the issue from Copilot, and then reassign the issue to Copilot.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tSelect Approve and run workflows in the pull request merge box.<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<span class=\"whead\">Proposed answers:</span><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"atbl\"><tr><th>Question part</th><th>Answer</th></tr><tr><td>Correct answer</td><td><b>C – Unassign the issue from Copilot, and then reassign the issue to Copilot.</b></td></tr></table></div><span class=\"whead\">Why:</span><ul><li><b>Stalled session</b>: no log or status updates for 70 minutes means the session is dead.</li><li><b>Reassign</b>: starts a new session from the same issue context and pushes new commits to the PR.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Why not the others:</span><ul><li><b>A – “Wait for the agent to complete.”</b>: a stalled session will not resume on its own.</li><li><b>B – “Merge the draft pull request and mention @copilot on the merged pull request.”</b>: ships unfinished work and closes the session; commenting on a merged PR does not restart it.</li><li><b>D – “Select Approve and run workflows in the pull request merge box.”</b>: only lets CI workflows run; it does not restart the agent.</li></ul><div class=\"learnbox\"><span class=\"whead\">Learn it:</span><ul><li><b>Concept</b>: A <b>coding agent session</b> is one run of the agent on an issue, shown by a draft PR and a session log. <b>Assigning</b> an issue to Copilot starts a session. Mentioning <code>@copilot</code> in a PR comment gives it follow-up instructions. <b>Approve and run workflows</b> lets pending Actions runs start for PRs from new contributors or bots.</li><li><b>How it works</b>: If a session has stopped, unassigning and reassigning the issue starts a new session that reads the same issue and works on it again. A stalled session will not recover by itself.</li><li><b>Example</b>: Issue #42 stalled for 70 minutes: remove Copilot from Assignees, add it again, and a new session begins.</li><li><b>Exam tip</b>: \"Stopped updating\" plus \"restart from the issue\" means unassign then reassign. Trap: merging the draft PR, which ships incomplete work and ends the PR.</li></ul></div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/coding-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot coding agent</a></li></ul>"},
  {"n":39,"topic":5,"q":9,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You have a GitHub Enterprise Cloud Organization that uses a custom coding agent to run GitHub Actions workflows that create branches, open pull requests, and merge changes after required checks pass.<br />\nYou need to log all agent-initiated actions and ensure that the logs are retained for two years.<br />\nWhat should you do?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tConfigure audit log retention.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tEnable log forwarding.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tEnable Git events in the audit log.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tConfigure audit log streaming to Azure Event Hubs.<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<span class=\"whead\">Proposed answers:</span><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"atbl\"><tr><th>Question part</th><th>Answer</th></tr><tr><td>Correct answer</td><td><b>D – Configure audit log streaming to Azure Event Hubs.</b></td></tr></table></div><span class=\"whead\">Why:</span><ul><li><b>Streaming</b>: sends every audit event to your own storage as it happens.</li><li><b>Your retention</b>: you control the sink, so you can keep the data for two years or more.</li><li><b>Agent actions</b>: branch, PR and merge actions are audit events, so they get streamed too.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Why not the others:</span><ul><li><b>A – “Configure audit log retention.”</b>: there is no setting that extends GitHub's own retention to two years.</li><li><b>B – “Enable log forwarding.”</b>: names no real destination or retention control.</li><li><b>C – “Enable Git events in the audit log.”</b>: only adds more event types; it does not make them last longer.</li></ul><div class=\"learnbox\"><span class=\"whead\">Learn it:</span><ul><li><b>Concept</b>: <b>Audit log retention</b> is how long GitHub itself keeps events (a limited period, not configurable to years). <b>Audit log streaming</b> (Enterprise Cloud) sends events in near real time to an external system such as Azure Event Hubs, Amazon S3, Splunk, Datadog or Google Cloud Storage. <b>Git events</b> are an optional extra event type in the audit log.</li><li><b>How it works</b>: Configure streaming in the enterprise settings (Audit log, Log streaming), choose a provider such as Azure Event Hubs, and give the connection details. After that, retention is controlled by your storage, so you can keep two years of logs.</li><li><b>Example</b>: Enterprise streams to Event Hubs, which archives to blob storage kept for 2 years; auditors query that archive.</li><li><b>Exam tip</b>: \"Retain for N years\" means stream to external storage. Trap: expecting a setting that extends GitHub-side retention, or confusing event types (Git events) with retention.</li></ul></div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/enterprise-cloud@latest/admin/monitoring-activity-in-your-enterprise/reviewing-audit-logs-for-your-enterprise/streaming-the-audit-log-for-your-enterprise\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Audit log streaming</a></li></ul>"},
  {"n":40,"topic":5,"q":10,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You have multiple GitHub Copilot coding agents that run tasks concurrently.<br />\nYou live stream the session log output and see the following.<br />\n<img title=\"image45\" src=\"images/image39.png\"><br />\nWhat is a possible cause of the error?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\ta postToolUse hook<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\ta GitHub app installation policy<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\ta preToolUse hook<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\ta Copilot firewall policy<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<span class=\"whead\">Proposed answers:</span><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"atbl\"><tr><th>Question part</th><th>Answer</th></tr><tr><td>Correct answer</td><td><b>C – a preToolUse hook</b></td></tr></table></div><span class=\"whead\">Result:</span><pre class=\"acode\">Run ./scripts/agent-run.sh\nrm -rf /infra\n<mark>Error: Command blocked by policy</mark>\n<mark>Reason: destructive_operation_detected</mark>\n[agent] Escalating to human review...</pre><span class=\"whead\">Why:</span><ul><li><b><code>preToolUse</code></b>: runs before a tool or command executes and can deny it.</li><li><b>The log</b>: <code>rm -rf /infra</code> was blocked by policy before running, then escalated to a human.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Why not the others:</span><ul><li><b>A – “a postToolUse hook”</b>: runs after a command finishes, so it cannot stop it beforehand.</li><li><b>B – “a GitHub app installation policy”</b>: controls which repos or permissions an app gets, not each command.</li><li><b>D – “a Copilot firewall policy”</b>: blocks network access and gives network-style errors, not a \"destructive operation\" message.</li></ul><div class=\"learnbox\"><span class=\"whead\">Learn it:</span><ul><li><b>Concept</b>: <b>Hooks</b> are scripts or policies that run at defined points of an agent session. <code>preToolUse</code> runs before a tool call and can approve or deny it. <code>postToolUse</code> runs after a tool call completes. A <b>firewall policy</b> limits which network hosts the agent can reach. A <b>GitHub App installation policy</b> controls which apps can be installed.</li><li><b>How it works</b>: Hooks are defined in JSON files such as <code>.github/hooks/*.json</code>. A <code>preToolUse</code> hook receives the tool name and arguments and can return a denial, so the command never runs and the agent gets an error.</li><li><b>Example</b>: A hook denies any command matching <code>rm -rf</code>; the log shows \"Command blocked by policy: destructive_operation_detected\" and the agent escalates to a human.</li><li><b>Exam tip</b>: A command is blocked before it runs, with a policy message, means <code>preToolUse</code>. Trap: firewall errors look like blocked hosts or timeouts, not blocked commands.</li></ul></div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/copilot-cli\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot CLI</a></li></ul>"},
  {"n":41,"topic":5,"q":11,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You are analyzing the following agent logs.<br />\n<img title=\"image46\" src=\"images/image40.png\"><br />\nYou need to classify the error in a report for your company's CTO.<br />\nHow should you classify the error?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tContext Issue<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tReasoning error<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tTool misuse<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tNetwork issue<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<span class=\"whead\">Proposed answers:</span><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"atbl\"><tr><th>Question part</th><th>Answer</th></tr><tr><td>Correct answer</td><td><b>C – Tool misuse</b></td></tr></table></div><span class=\"whead\">Result:</span><pre class=\"acode\"><mark>Error from tool mcp_microsoftdocs_microsoft_docs_search</mark> with args {\"query\":\"...\"}:\n<mark>Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'invoke')</mark>: TypeError</pre><span class=\"whead\">Why:</span><ul><li><b>Tool call failed</b>: the error is raised by the MCP docs search tool itself.</li><li><b>No result</b>: the call broke while being invoked (<code>reading 'invoke'</code>), so nothing came back.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Why not the others:</span><ul><li><b>A – “Context Issue”</b>: would be about prompt size or the wrong files loaded; the other calls succeeded.</li><li><b>B – “Reasoning error”</b>: would be a working call with a wrong decision; here the call itself failed.</li><li><b>D – “Network issue”</b>: would show timeouts, DNS or connection errors, not a <code>TypeError</code>.</li></ul><div class=\"learnbox\"><span class=\"whead\">Learn it:</span><ul><li><b>Concept</b>: Agent errors fall into types. <b>Tool misuse</b>: a tool call fails or is used wrongly. <b>Reasoning error</b>: the agent decides wrongly even though tools work. <b>Context issue</b>: missing, too much, or wrong information given to the model. <b>Network issue</b>: connectivity failures such as DNS, TLS or timeouts.</li><li><b>How it works</b>: Look at where the error appears. Here it is <code>Error from tool mcp_microsoftdocs_microsoft_docs_search</code> with a <code>TypeError</code> when invoking the tool, so the tool call itself broke. The surrounding model calls say <code>success</code>.</li><li><b>Example</b>: If the docs search returned results but the agent picked the wrong page, that would be a reasoning error instead.</li><li><b>Exam tip</b>: An \"Error from tool X\" line means tool misuse. Trap: calling any error a network issue; a <code>TypeError</code> is a code error, not a connectivity error.</li></ul></div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/coding-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot coding agent</a></li><li><a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/copilot/customization/mcp-servers\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MCP servers in VS Code</a></li></ul>"},
  {"n":42,"topic":5,"q":12,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You have multiple GitHub Copilot coding agents that run tasks concurrently.<br />\nYou are monitoring the agents from the terminal by using the GitHub CLI.<br />\nAn agent appears stalled.<br />\nYou need to live stream the session log output.<br />\nWhat should you do?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tRun gh agent-task view and specify the --log and --jq parameters.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tRun gh agent-task view and specify the --log and --follow parameters.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tRun gh agent-task list and specify the --web parameter.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tRun gh agent-task list and specify the --status and --jq parameters.<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<span class=\"whead\">Proposed answers:</span><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"atbl\"><tr><th>Question part</th><th>Answer</th></tr><tr><td>Correct answer</td><td><b>B – Run gh agent-task view and specify the --log and --follow parameters.</b></td></tr></table></div><span class=\"whead\">Result:</span><pre class=\"acode\">gh agent-task view 42 <mark>--log --follow</mark></pre><span class=\"whead\">Why:</span><ul><li><b><code>view</code></b>: targets one agent task.</li><li><b><code>--log</code></b>: prints that task's session log.</li><li><b><code>--follow</code></b>: keeps streaming new output, like <code>tail -f</code>.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Why not the others:</span><ul><li><b>A – “Run gh agent-task view and specify the --log and --jq parameters.”</b>: <code>--jq</code> filters JSON; it does not stream.</li><li><b>C – “Run gh agent-task list and specify the --web parameter.”</b>: lists tasks and opens a browser; no live log in the terminal.</li><li><b>D – “Run gh agent-task list and specify the --status and --jq parameters.”</b>: only lists and filters tasks; no log.</li></ul><div class=\"learnbox\"><span class=\"whead\">Learn it:</span><ul><li><b>Concept</b>: <code>gh agent-task</code> is a GitHub CLI command group for coding agent tasks. <code>list</code> shows tasks, <code>view</code> shows one task, <code>--log</code> shows its session log, <code>--follow</code> streams it live, <code>--jq</code> filters JSON output, <code>--web</code> opens the browser.</li><li><b>How it works</b>: Combine a verb plus flags: <code>gh agent-task view &lt;id-or-pr&gt; --log --follow</code> prints the log and keeps updating until you press Ctrl+C. <code>list</code> accepts filters but never prints a task log.</li><li><b>Example</b>: <code>gh agent-task view 42 --log --follow</code> to watch a stalled task.</li><li><b>Exam tip</b>: \"Live stream\" means <code>--follow</code>; \"session log\" means <code>view --log</code>. Trap: <code>--jq</code> and <code>--web</code> never stream.</li></ul></div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/coding-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot coding agent</a></li></ul>"},
  {"n":43,"topic":5,"q":13,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You have a GitHub Copilot coding agent that has completed a pull request for a security fix in your repository.<br />\nBefore merging, you need to evaluate the quality of the agent's work by using both automated evaluation signals and human review.<br />\nYou review the session log and the pull request.<br />\nWhat are two automated evaluation signals generated by the coding agent's built-in scanning tools? Each correct answer presents a complete solution.<br><br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tlinting errors from the repository's CI pipeline configured in GitHub Actions<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tcomments from Copilot code review that suggest improvements to code patterns<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tthe detection of hardcoded secrets, such as API keys and tokens<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tthe median time to merge metric reported on the Copilot usage metrics dashboard<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>E.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tCodeQL findings that identify security vulnerabilities in the generated code<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<span class=\"whead\">Proposed answers:</span><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"atbl\"><tr><th>Question part</th><th>Answer</th></tr><tr><td>Correct answer 1</td><td><b>C – the detection of hardcoded secrets, such as API keys and tokens</b></td></tr><tr><td>Correct answer 2</td><td><b>E – CodeQL findings that identify security vulnerabilities in the generated code</b></td></tr></table></div><span class=\"whead\">Why:</span><ul><li><b>Secret scanning (C)</b>: built-in check that flags API keys and tokens in the agent's code.</li><li><b>CodeQL (E)</b>: built-in code scanning that finds security vulnerabilities in the generated code.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Why not the others:</span><ul><li><b>A – “linting errors from the repository's CI pipeline configured in GitHub Actions”</b>: comes from your own Actions pipeline, not the agent's built-in tools.</li><li><b>B – “comments from Copilot code review that suggest improvements to code patterns”</b>: are AI suggestions, not scanner results.</li><li><b>D – “the median time to merge metric reported on the Copilot usage metrics dashboard”</b>: is a process metric, not a check on this code.</li></ul><div class=\"learnbox\"><span class=\"whead\">Learn it:</span><ul><li><b>Concept</b>: <b>Secret scanning</b> finds credentials like API keys and tokens in code. <b>CodeQL</b> is GitHub's code analysis engine that finds vulnerabilities (injection, weak auth). Both run automatically on the coding agent's code as built-in validation. <b>CI linting</b>, <b>Copilot code review comments</b> and <b>time-to-merge</b> are separate things.</li><li><b>How it works</b>: Before the agent finishes, it runs its own checks (CodeQL, secret scanning, dependency checks) on its changes and fixes problems it finds. You still review the session log and PR yourself.</li><li><b>Example</b>: The agent adds <code>const key = \"sk_live_...\"</code>: secret scanning flags it. It builds SQL by string concatenation: CodeQL flags the injection.</li><li><b>Exam tip</b>: \"Built-in scanning tools\" means secret scanning and CodeQL (code scanning). Trap: choosing your own CI lint or AI review comments, which are not built-in scanners.</li></ul></div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/code-security/secret-scanning/about-secret-scanning\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Secret scanning</a></li><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/code-security/code-scanning/introduction-to-code-scanning/about-code-scanning\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Code scanning / CodeQL</a></li></ul>"},
  {"n":44,"topic":5,"q":14,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You have a GitHub Enterprise repository that runs an autonomous agent by using a GitHub Actions workflow. The workflow has the following jobs:<br />\nagent-run that generates trace.json and plan.md<br />\nreview that waits for human approval before continuing<br />\ndeploy that uses the outputs from agent-run<br />\nYou need to make the files inspectable in the GitHub Actions UI and ensure that the files are available to the review and deploy jobs.<br />\nWhat should you do in the workflow?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tUse dependency caching to store trace.json and plan.md.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tUpload trace.json and plan.md as workflow artifacts in agent-run, and download the files inside the jobs.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tCommit trace.json and plan.md back to the repository from agent-run.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tStore trace.json and plan.md on a network share and have later jobs retrieve them from the share.<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<span class=\"whead\">Proposed answers:</span><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"atbl\"><tr><th>Question part</th><th>Answer</th></tr><tr><td>Correct answer</td><td><b>B – Upload trace.json and plan.md as workflow artifacts in agent-run, and download the files inside the jobs.</b></td></tr></table></div><span class=\"whead\">Result:</span><pre class=\"acode\">agent-run:\n  <mark>- uses: actions/upload-artifact@v4</mark>\n      with: { name: agent-output, path: \"trace.json\\nplan.md\" }\nreview / deploy:\n  <mark>- uses: actions/download-artifact@v4</mark></pre><span class=\"whead\">Why:</span><ul><li><b>Artifacts</b>: show up in the Actions run UI, so people can inspect the files.</li><li><b>Job handoff</b>: each job runs on a fresh runner, so files only pass between jobs through upload then download.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Why not the others:</span><ul><li><b>A – “Use dependency caching to store trace.json and plan.md.”</b>: is best-effort and meant for dependencies; it can miss and is not for inspecting files.</li><li><b>C – “Commit trace.json and plan.md back to the repository from agent-run.”</b>: fills history with generated files.</li><li><b>D – “Store trace.json and plan.md on a network share and have later jobs retrieve them from the share.”</b>: extra infrastructure the runners may not reach.</li></ul><div class=\"learnbox\"><span class=\"whead\">Learn it:</span><ul><li><b>Concept</b>: A <b>workflow artifact</b> is a file or folder saved from a job and kept with the workflow run. A <b>cache</b> speeds up runs by reusing dependencies. A <b>job</b> runs on its own fresh runner, so files do not carry over to the next job.</li><li><b>How it works</b>: In the first job use <code>actions/upload-artifact</code>; in later jobs add <code>needs: agent-run</code> and use <code>actions/download-artifact</code>. Uploaded artifacts appear on the workflow run summary page where people can download and inspect them. Default retention is 90 days.</li><li><b>Example</b>: <code>agent-run</code> uploads <code>plan.md</code>; <code>review</code> downloads it so the approver can read it; <code>deploy</code> downloads it too.</li><li><b>Exam tip</b>: \"Pass files between jobs\" and \"inspect in the UI\" both mean artifacts. Trap: cache (dependencies only) and committing outputs to the repo.</li></ul></div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/actions/using-workflows/storing-workflow-data-as-artifacts\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Workflow artifacts</a></li></ul>"},
  {"n":45,"topic":5,"q":15,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  A developer uses the GitHub Copilot CLI in plan mode.<br />\nCopilot produces a plan.<br />\nWhat does Copilot do next?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tsaves the plan to plan.md<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tcreates a branch named /copilot/plan in the repository<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\topens a pull request that has the plan as a comment<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tbegins the implementation<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<span class=\"whead\">Proposed answers:</span><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"atbl\"><tr><th>Question part</th><th>Answer</th></tr><tr><td>Correct answer</td><td><b>A. saves the plan to plan.md</b></td></tr></table></div><span class=\"whead\">Why:</span><ul><li><b>Plan mode</b>: means \"think, don't act\". Copilot researches and writes the plan.</li><li><b>plan.md</b>: is the deliverable: you read it, edit it, and approve it.</li><li><b>Stops</b>: after writing. Nothing else happens until you give the next instruction.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Why not the others:</span><ul><li><b>B. creates a branch named /copilot/plan in the repository</b>: creating a branch already changes the repo.</li><li><b>C. opens a pull request that has the plan as a comment</b>: publishing a PR before you approve the plan is acting, not planning.</li><li><b>D. begins the implementation</b>: the opposite of plan mode.</li></ul><div class=\"learnbox\"><span class=\"whead\">Learn it:</span><ul><li><b>Concept</b>: <b>Plan mode</b> in Copilot CLI: Copilot analyzes the task and writes a step-by-step plan, without changing code yet.</li><li><b>Concept</b>: <b>Normal mode</b>: Copilot acts directly (edits files, runs commands) as it works.</li><li><b>Concept</b>: <b>Branch / pull request</b>: Git actions that publish or isolate changes; they are implementation-side steps, not planning.</li><li><b>How it works</b>: In the CLI you switch modes (Shift+Tab cycles modes). In plan mode the plan is saved as a file (<code>plan.md</code>) that you can read and edit, and Copilot waits for your go-ahead before implementing.</li><li><b>Example</b>: You ask for \"migrate login to OAuth\" in plan mode, review <code>plan.md</code>, tweak two steps, then tell Copilot to implement.</li><li><b>Exam tip</b>: The words \"plan mode\" plus \"what next\" point to \"saves the plan and stops\". Options that create branches, PRs or start coding are the trap.</li></ul></div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/copilot-cli\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot CLI</a></li></ul>"},
  {"n":46,"topic":6,"q":1,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  Case study<br><br>Existing Environment<br />\nGitHub Environment<br />\nThe GitHub environment contains the following:<br />\nThree repositories named product-api, billing-service, and infra-terraform.<br />\nBranch protection on the main branch in all repositories that requires at least one pull request review before merging<br />\nGitHub Actions runners used across all workflows<br />\nA GitHub team named SG_Dev that contains developers<br />\nA GitHub team named SG_Review that contains senior engineers and a security team<br />\nA .github/copilot-instructions.md file that includes general coding conventions for all features<br />\nAgent environment<br />\nThe product-api repository uses a GitHub Copilot coding agent named agent1 that has the following configurations:<br />\nNo custom agent profile is defined.<br />\nA Model Context Protocol (MCP) server named MCP1 is deployed to https://mcp.litwareinc.internal and provides access to internal ticketing and deployment APIs. MCP1 requires an API key for authentication.<br />\nA second Copilot coding agent named agent2 handles changes in infra-terraform and runs in parallel with agent1 when both agents have open assigned issues.<br />\nCopilot memory is NOT enabled for the organization.<br />\nProblem Statements<br />\nLitware identifies the following issues:<br />\nDuring two recent sessions, agent1 accessed files in billing-service, which is outside the agent's intended scope.<br />\nagent1 makes code changes immediately after receiving a task.<br />\nA developer named Ben, who is on the SG_Dev team, reports that agent1 completed a session with a successful status and opened a pull request, but the pull request contains no file changes. Other developers report this intermittently as well.<br />\nBoth agent1 and agent2 modified shared/config.yaml in a parallel test run, generating conflicting outputs.<br />\nagent1 consistently uses raw try-catch blocks for error handling, which violates the defined implementation guidelines of SG_Dev.<br />\nRequirements<br />\nPlanned Changes<br />\nLitware plans to make the following changes:<br />\nEnsure that agent1 can access all the tools in the environment.<br />\nProvide product-api with specific instructions to agent1 without affecting Copilot Chat or Copilot code review.<br />\nConfigure MCP1 as a tool for agent1 by modifying the product-api repository MCP configuration.<br />\nEnsure that Copilot retains details that it has learned and uses that knowledge for future work. This must be applied to all licensed members of the organization.<br />\nImplementation guidelines<br />\nThe development team at Litware identifies the following implementation guidelines:<br />\nAgent workflows must be able to run in parallel.<br />\nApplication error handling must use the repository ErrorHandler class.<br />\nagent1 and agent2 must run on isolated branches during parallel execution. File-level conflicts must be detected before merges, and both agents must be able to run concurrently.<br />\nSecurity requirements<br />\nLitware identifies the following security requirements:<br />\nOnly the members of SG_Review must be able to approve agent1 plan outputs.<br />\nAll API keys must be stored and accessed securely.<br />\nThe developers must NOT be able to self-approve.<br />\nAgent configuration<br />\n<img title=\"image47\" src=\"images/image10.png\"><br />\nYou need to resolve the issue of the agents generating conflicting output. The solution must meet the implementation guidelines.<br />\nWhat should you do?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tAdd shared/config.yaml to a CODEOWNERS file that requires SG_Review approval before any changes can be merged.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tConfigure each agent to work on a separate branch and add a required status check that detects file-level overlap before either pull request can be merged.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tConfigure tools: ['read', 'search'] in both agent profiles to prevent either agent from writing files.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tConfigure a concurrency group on both agent workflows so that only one workflow runs at a time.<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<span class=\"whead\">Proposed answers:</span><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"atbl\"><tr><th>Question part</th><th>Answer</th></tr><tr><td>Correct answer</td><td><b>B. Configure each agent to work on a separate branch and add a required status check that detects file-level overlap before either pull request can be merged.</b></td></tr></table></div><span class=\"whead\">Why:</span><ul><li><b>Separate branch</b>: each agent edits in its own workspace (isolated branches).</li><li><b>Required status check</b>: fails a PR if both touch the same file, so the conflict is caught before merge.</li><li><b>Still parallel</b>: both agents keep running at the same time, as required.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Why not the others:</span><ul><li><b>A. Add shared/config.yaml to a CODEOWNERS file that requires SG_Review approval before any changes can be merged.</b>: adds a reviewer but does not isolate the work or detect overlap.</li><li><b>C. Configure tools: ['read', 'search'] in both agent profiles to prevent either agent from writing files.</b>: agents could no longer write code, so they cannot do their job.</li><li><b>D. Configure a concurrency group on both agent workflows so that only one workflow runs at a time.</b>: runs one at a time, which breaks the \"run concurrently\" requirement.</li></ul><div class=\"learnbox\"><span class=\"whead\">Learn it:</span><ul><li><b>Concept</b>: <b>Isolated branch</b>: each agent works on its own Git branch, so their edits never mix while running.</li><li><b>Concept</b>: <b>Required status check</b>: a check that must pass before a PR can merge (set in branch protection or a ruleset). A custom check can compare changed files between PRs.</li><li><b>Concept</b>: <b>CODEOWNERS</b>: file that assigns required reviewers to paths. <b>Concurrency group</b>: Actions setting that limits runs sharing a key to one at a time.</li><li><b>How it works</b>: Each agent opens its own PR from its own branch. A workflow marked as required runs on both PRs and fails if the same file (for example <code>shared/config.yaml</code>) is changed in both. Conflicts are then fixed before merge.</li><li><b>Example</b>: Agent1 and agent2 both edit <code>shared/config.yaml</code>; the overlap check fails both PRs, a human reconciles the file, then both merge.</li><li><b>Exam tip</b>: Look at every requirement (parallel, isolated, detect conflicts, concurrent). Each wrong option breaks one: read-only breaks work, concurrency group breaks concurrency, CODEOWNERS breaks isolation/detection.</li></ul></div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/coding-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot coding agent</a></li><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/configuring-branches-and-merges-in-your-repository/managing-protected-branches/about-protected-branches\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Branch protection rules</a></li></ul>"},
  {"n":47,"topic":6,"q":2,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  HOTSPOT<br />\nYou have a GitHub repository that contains the following custom agent files:<br />\nA file named planner.agent.md that includes YAML frontmatter with a handoffs entry that has label: Start Implementation, agent: implementer, and prompt: Now implement the plan outlined above<br />\nA file named implementer.agent.md that is in the same directory as planner.agent.md and includes YAML frontmatter that has name: IMPLEMENTER<br />\nYou add a third agent file named review.agent.md.review.agent.md includes YAML frontmatter that has name: code-review.<br />\nYou make the following changes to planner.agent.md:<br />\nUpdate the existing handoff to include send: true and model: GPT-5.2 (copilot).<br />\nAdd a second handoff that has label: Run Review, agent: code-review, and prompt: Review the code changes made in the previous step.<br />\nNo other agent files are modified.<br />\nFor each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.<br />\n<br />\n<img title=\"image48\" src=\"images/image41.png\"><br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<span class=\"whead\">Proposed answers:</span><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"atbl\"><tr><th>#</th><th>Statement</th><th>Answer</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>The Run Review handoff resolves to review.agent.md.</td><td><b>No</b></td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>The Start Implementation handoff fails to identify the target agent.</td><td><b>No</b></td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Selecting Start Implementation switches the chat to the implementer agent and automatically submits the handoff prompt to that agent by using GPT-5.2 (copilot).</td><td><b>Yes</b></td></tr></table></div><span class=\"whead\">Result:</span><pre class=\"acode\">handoffs:\n  - label: Start Implementation\n<mark>    agent: implementer</mark>\n     prompt: Now implement the plan outlined above\n<mark>    send: true</mark>\n     model: GPT-5.2 (copilot)\n  - label: Run Review\n<mark>    agent: code-review</mark></pre><span class=\"whead\">Why:</span><ul><li><b>1 = No</b>: <code>agent:</code> must match the file name (<code>code-review.agent.md</code>), not the <code>name:</code> inside <code>review.agent.md</code>. No such file exists, so it does not resolve to <code>review.agent.md</code>.</li><li><b>2 = No</b>: <code>implementer</code> matches <code>implementer.agent.md</code>, so the target is found.</li><li><b>3 = Yes</b>: <code>send: true</code> submits the prompt automatically, and <code>model</code> sets GPT-5.2 (copilot).</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Why not the other values:</span><ul><li><b>1 = Yes</b>: would need the frontmatter name (<code>code-review</code>) to count as an identifier; it does not.</li><li><b>2 = Yes</b>: would be true only if the file name did not match; it does.</li><li><b>3 = No</b>: would apply only with <code>send: false</code>, which shows a button instead.</li></ul><div class=\"learnbox\"><span class=\"whead\">Learn it:</span><ul><li><b>Concept</b>: <b>Handoff</b>: a button in a custom agent that moves the chat to another agent with a prefilled prompt.</li><li><b>Concept</b>: <b><code>agent</code></b>: the target agent identifier (the agent file name without <code>.agent.md</code>). <b><code>name</code></b> in frontmatter is just the display name.</li><li><b>Concept</b>: <b><code>send</code></b>: <code>true</code> submits the prompt automatically; default <code>false</code> only fills it in. <b><code>model</code></b>: optional model for the handoff, written like <code>GPT-5.2 (copilot)</code>.</li><li><b>How it works</b>: Custom agents live in <code>.github/agents/*.agent.md</code> (or the VS Code agents folder). Handoffs are defined under <code>handoffs:</code> in the YAML frontmatter with <code>label</code>, <code>agent</code>, <code>prompt</code>, <code>send</code>, <code>model</code>.</li><li><b>Example</b>: <code>agent: code-review</code> looks for <code>code-review.agent.md</code>. The file is <code>review.agent.md</code>, so it is not found even though its <code>name:</code> is code-review.</li><li><b>Exam tip</b>: Statements that mix \"name\" and \"file name\" are the trap: the exam wants you to match the identifier to the file name. Check <code>send</code> for the auto-submit statement.</li></ul></div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/agent-customization/custom-agents\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Custom agents in VS Code (incl. handoffs)</a></li></ul>"},
  {"n":48,"topic":6,"q":3,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  HOTSPOT<br />\nYou have a GitHub repository that uses GitHub Actions to validate pull requests opened by the GitHub Copilot coding agent. The workflow runs unit tests and a linter on pull request triggers, and Copilot opens draft pull requests on dedicated branches while iterating by using commits.<br />\nYou discover that when multiple Copilot sessions push updates to the same pull request branch in quick succession, multiple workflow runs execute concurrently.<br />\nYou need to enable parallel workflow executions across different pull request branches.<br />\nHow should you configure workflow-level concurrency? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.<br />\n<br />\n<img title=\"image50\" src=\"images/image43.png\"><br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<span class=\"whead\">Proposed answers:</span><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"atbl\"><tr><th>#</th><th>Target</th><th>Value</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Concurrency scope:</td><td><b>Set concurrency at the workflow level.</b></td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Concurrency expression:</td><td><b>group: ${{ github.workflow }}-${{ github.ref }}</b></td></tr></table></div><span class=\"whead\">Result:</span><pre class=\"acode\">concurrency:\n<mark>  group: ${{ github.workflow }}-${{ github.ref }}</mark></pre><span class=\"whead\">Why:</span><ul><li><b>Set concurrency at the workflow level.</b>: one group covers the whole run, so rapid pushes to the same PR line up.</li><li><b><code>github.ref</code></b>: differs per branch, so each PR gets its own queue and different PRs run in parallel.</li><li><b><code>github.workflow</code></b>: keeps different workflows in separate queues.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Why not the others (scope):</span><ul><li><b>Set concurrency at each job level only.</b>: runs from rapid pushes still overlap.</li><li><b>Set strategy.matrix to limit parallelism.</b>: matrix fans out jobs, it does not group runs.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Why not the others (expression):</span><ul><li><b>group: ${{ github.head_ref || github.run_id }}</b>: <code>run_id</code> is unique per run, so non-PR runs are never grouped.</li><li><b>group: ${{ github.ref }}</b>: groups by branch, but without the workflow name different workflows on the same branch would block each other.</li><li><b>group: ${{ github.run_id }}</b>: unique per run, so nothing is ever grouped.</li></ul><div class=\"learnbox\"><span class=\"whead\">Learn it:</span><ul><li><b>Concept</b>: <b>Concurrency</b> in Actions: a <code>concurrency</code> key with a <code>group</code>; only one run per group runs at a time.</li><li><b>Concept</b>: <b><code>github.ref</code></b>: the branch or PR ref that triggered the run. <b><code>github.run_id</code></b>: unique number per run.</li><li><b>Concept</b>: <b><code>strategy.matrix</code></b>: runs one job several times with different variables; unrelated to grouping runs. <b>Job level</b>: concurrency on a single job instead of the whole workflow.</li><li><b>How it works</b>: Runs with the same group value wait for each other (a newer pending run replaces an older pending one; add <code>cancel-in-progress: true</code> to cancel the running one). Different values run in parallel.</li><li><b>Example</b>: Pushes to <code>refs/heads/copilot/fix-1</code> queue behind each other, while <code>copilot/fix-2</code> runs at the same time.</li><li><b>Exam tip</b>: To be parallel across branches but serial within a branch, the key must contain the ref. A key with <code>run_id</code> is always unique, so it groups nothing.</li></ul></div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/actions/using-jobs/using-concurrency\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Workflow concurrency groups</a></li></ul>"},
  {"n":49,"topic":6,"q":4,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You have a GitHub repository that contains an agent named Orchestrator.Orchestrator delegates work to the following specialized subagents:<br />\nPlanner reviews issues and creates a plan of action.<br />\nImplementer writes code based on the plan of action.<br />\nReviewer reviews the code.<br />\nYou create a new agent named Summarizer that produces a concise summary of the work performed by the other agents.<br />\nYou need to ensure that Orchestrator can invoke Summarizer as part of its workflow.<br />\nWhat should you do?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tIn the YAML frontmatter of the Reviewer agent, add a handoffs entry that points to the Summarizer agent.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tIn the YAML frontmatter of the Reviewer agent, add Summarizer to the agents list.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tIn the YAML frontmatter of the Orchestrator agent, add Summarizer to the tools list.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tIn the YAML frontmatter of the Orchestrator agent, add Summarizer to the agents list.<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<span class=\"whead\">Proposed answers:</span><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"atbl\"><tr><th>Question part</th><th>Answer</th></tr><tr><td>Correct answer</td><td><b>D. In the YAML frontmatter of the Orchestrator agent, add Summarizer to the agents list.</b></td></tr></table></div><span class=\"whead\">Result:</span><pre class=\"acode\"># orchestrator.agent.md\n<mark>agents: [Planner, Implementer, Reviewer, Summarizer]</mark></pre><span class=\"whead\">Why:</span><ul><li><b>Orchestrator calls the subagents</b>: so its own <code>agents</code> list must name every agent it may call.</li><li><b>Listed = callable</b>: Summarizer is only invocable once it appears there.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Why not the others:</span><ul><li><b>A. In the YAML frontmatter of the Reviewer agent, add a handoffs entry that points to the Summarizer agent.</b>: handoffs are chat buttons for people, and Reviewer is not the caller.</li><li><b>B. In the YAML frontmatter of the Reviewer agent, add Summarizer to the agents list.</b>: wrong agent; Reviewer does not invoke Summarizer.</li><li><b>C. In the YAML frontmatter of the Orchestrator agent, add Summarizer to the tools list.</b>: tools hold tools, not agents.</li></ul><div class=\"learnbox\"><span class=\"whead\">Learn it:</span><ul><li><b>Concept</b>: <b>Orchestrator pattern</b>: one main agent delegates work to specialized subagents.</li><li><b>Concept</b>: <b><code>agents</code></b> frontmatter list: the subagents this agent is allowed to call. <b><code>handoffs</code></b>: buttons a person clicks to switch agents.</li><li><b>Concept</b>: <b><code>tools</code></b> list: tools (like <code>search</code>, <code>edit</code>), not agents.</li><li><b>How it works</b>: In the caller's <code>.agent.md</code> frontmatter, list subagent names under <code>agents:</code>. The model can then run them as part of a task and gets the result back.</li><li><b>Example</b>: <code>agents: [Planner, Implementer, Reviewer, Summarizer]</code> in the Orchestrator file.</li><li><b>Exam tip</b>: Ask: who calls whom? The edit goes in the caller (Orchestrator), in the <code>agents</code> list. \"Handoff\" and \"tools\" are the distractors.</li></ul></div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/agent-customization/custom-agents\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Custom agents in VS Code</a></li></ul>"},
  {"n":50,"topic":6,"q":5,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You have a multi-agent GitHub Actions workflow that uploads review artifacts for each run.<br />\nYou discover that some workflow run artifacts are being deleted manually.<br />\nYou need to use your organization's audit log data to identify which user deleted the artifacts.<br />\nWhich audit log search filter should you use?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\trepo:&lt;org&gt;/&lt;repo&gt;<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\taction:workflows.run<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\toperation:remove<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\taction:artifact.destroy<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<span class=\"whead\">Proposed answers:</span><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"atbl\"><tr><th>Question part</th><th>Answer</th></tr><tr><td>Correct answer</td><td><b>D. action:artifact.destroy</b></td></tr></table></div><span class=\"whead\">Why:</span><ul><li><b>action:</b>: filters the audit log by event type.</li><li><b>artifact.destroy</b>: is the event written when an artifact is deleted, and it shows who did it.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Why not the others:</span><ul><li><b>A. repo:&lt;org&gt;/&lt;repo&gt;</b>: filters by repository, not by what happened.</li><li><b>B. action:workflows.run</b>: finds workflow runs, not deletions.</li><li><b>C. operation:remove</b>: not a valid audit log filter.</li></ul><div class=\"learnbox\"><span class=\"whead\">Learn it:</span><ul><li><b>Concept</b>: <b>Audit log</b>: record of who did what in an org or enterprise, with actor and timestamp.</li><li><b>Concept</b>: <b><code>action:</code></b> filter: event type, written as <code>category.verb</code> (for example <code>artifact.destroy</code>).</li><li><b>Concept</b>: <b><code>repo:</code></b> filter: limits results to one repository. <code>workflows.*</code> events describe workflow runs, not artifact deletion.</li><li><b>How it works</b>: In Settings, Audit log, type a search like <code>action:artifact.destroy</code>; results show the actor (user) and time. You can combine filters, for example add <code>repo:org/repo</code>.</li><li><b>Example</b>: <code>action:artifact.destroy repo:contoso/portal</code> lists who deleted artifacts in that repo.</li><li><b>Exam tip</b>: \"Which user deleted X\" means filter on the delete event with <code>action:</code>. Filters that are not real syntax (like <code>operation:remove</code>) are traps.</li></ul></div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/enterprise-cloud@latest/admin/monitoring-activity-in-your-enterprise/reviewing-audit-logs-for-your-enterprise/streaming-the-audit-log-for-your-enterprise\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Audit log streaming</a></li></ul>"},
  {"n":51,"topic":6,"q":6,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You have a GitHub Copilot coding agent named Orchestrator that runs a multi-phase workflow by using the following subagents:<br />\nExplorer gathers context by using read-only tools.<br />\nModifier applies focused edits.<br />\nYou are adding a new agent named Summarizer that generates a concise summary after modifications are complete. Summarizer includes the following YAML frontmatter.<br />\n<img title=\"image52\" src=\"images/image45.png\"><br />\nThe Orchestrator agent lists all three agents in its agents property.<br />\nAfter adding the Summarizer agent, Orchestrator successfully runs Explorer and Modifier but fails to run Summarizer.<br />\nWhat is a possible cause of the failure?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tSummarizer cannot be invoked as a subagent because disable-model-invocation is set to true.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tOrchestrator is missing a handoff entry to trigger Summarizer.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tOrchestrator cannot call Summarizer because user-invocable is set to false.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tSummarizer is missing the editing tools required to complete the workflow.<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<span class=\"whead\">Proposed answers:</span><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"atbl\"><tr><th>Question part</th><th>Answer</th></tr><tr><td>Correct answer</td><td><b>A. Summarizer cannot be invoked as a subagent because disable-model-invocation is set to true.</b></td></tr></table></div><span class=\"whead\">Result:</span><pre class=\"acode\">name: Summarizer\ndescription: Produce a concise summary of recent changes\ntools: ['fetch']\nuser-invocable: false\n<mark>disable-model-invocation: true</mark></pre><span class=\"whead\">Why:</span><ul><li><b>disable-model-invocation: true</b>: tells the model it may not call this agent, even if it is in the <code>agents</code> list.</li><li><b>Siblings work</b>: Explorer and Modifier run, so the <code>agents</code> list is fine. Only Summarizer has the blocking flag.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Why not the others:</span><ul><li><b>B. Orchestrator is missing a handoff entry to trigger Summarizer.</b>: handoffs are chat buttons; subagent calls use the <code>agents</code> list.</li><li><b>C. Orchestrator cannot call Summarizer because user-invocable is set to false.</b>: only stops people from picking it in chat, not the model.</li><li><b>D. Summarizer is missing the editing tools required to complete the workflow.</b>: a summary only needs <code>fetch</code>; missing tools do not stop it starting.</li></ul><div class=\"note\"><b>Note:</b> Some community answers pick B, but the official answer is A.</div><div class=\"learnbox\"><span class=\"whead\">Learn it:</span><ul><li><b>Concept</b>: <b><code>agents</code></b> list on the caller: which subagents may be used.</li><li><b>Concept</b>: <b><code>user-invocable: false</code></b>: hides the agent from the chat picker, so people cannot select it, but other agents can still call it.</li><li><b>Concept</b>: <b><code>disable-model-invocation: true</code></b>: prevents the model (another agent) from calling it automatically.</li><li><b>How it works</b>: Both flags are in the subagent's own frontmatter. A subagent can be callable by people, by the model, by both or by neither, depending on these two flags.</li><li><b>Example</b>: Summarizer has <code>user-invocable: false</code> and <code>disable-model-invocation: true</code>, so nobody can start it; the Orchestrator listing does not override that.</li><li><b>Exam tip</b>: If siblings work but one agent fails, compare the frontmatter of the failing agent. \"Model invocation\" matches \"Orchestrator (a model) calls it\".</li></ul></div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/agent-customization/custom-agents\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Custom agents in VS Code</a></li></ul>"},
  {"n":52,"topic":6,"q":7,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  DRAG DROP<br />\nYou have a GitHub repository that runs an agentic software development lifecycle (SDLC) workflow by using GitHub Actions. The workflow uses the following three executors implemented as scripts: spec_analyzer, risk_reviewer, and plan_merger.<br />\nYou need to coordinate multiple specialized agents so that analysis and risk review run in parallel, and then a final executor merges the outputs into a single plan. The orchestration pattern must fan out one request to multiple executors, and then fan in the results to a final executor.<br />\nHow should you complete the workflow definition? To answer, drag the appropriate values to the correct targets. Each value may be used once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.<br />\n<br />\n<img title=\"image53\" src=\"images/image46.png\"><br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<span class=\"whead\">Proposed answers:</span><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"atbl\"><tr><th>#</th><th>Target</th><th>Value</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>plan_merger: key on the blank line after <code>runs-on: ubuntu-latest</code> (left box, before the colon)</td><td><b>needs</b></td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>plan_merger: value after that colon (right box)</td><td><b>[spec_analyzer, risk_reviewer]</b></td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Box above <code>group: multiagent-${{ github.ref }}</code> (before the colon)</td><td><b>concurrency</b></td></tr></table></div><span class=\"whead\">Result:</span><pre class=\"acode\">  plan_merger:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n<mark>    needs: [spec_analyzer, risk_reviewer]</mark>\n    steps:\n      - name: Merge executor outputs into a single plan\n        run: ./executors/plan_merger.sh\n      - name: Publish merged plan\n        run: echo \"publish plan artifact\"\n<mark>    concurrency:</mark>\n      group: multiagent-${{ github.ref }}</pre><span class=\"whead\">Why:</span><ul><li><b>needs</b>: makes <code>plan_merger</code> wait for both analyzers: they run in parallel (fan-out), then merge (fan-in).</li><li><b>[spec_analyzer, risk_reviewer]</b>: the two jobs whose output is merged.</li><li><b>concurrency</b>: is the key that owns <code>group:</code>; per-branch group serializes pushes to one branch.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Why not the other values (available: [spec_analyzer, risk_reviewer], concurrency, group, matrix, needs, strategy, tools):</span><ul><li><b>strategy / matrix</b>: repeat one job with variants, they do not order separate jobs.</li><li><b>group</b>: is a sub-key of <code>concurrency</code>, not the parent key.</li><li><b>tools</b>: belongs to agent frontmatter, not Actions YAML.</li></ul><div class=\"note\"><b>Note:</b> The image repeats the <code>spec_analyzer</code> job (a typo); it does not change the answer. The image does not show exactly where the blocks sit in the job; placement shown here follows the order of the blanks.</div><div class=\"learnbox\"><span class=\"whead\">Learn it:</span><ul><li><b>Concept</b>: <b>Fan-out / fan-in</b>: split work into parallel jobs, then join their results in one job.</li><li><b>Concept</b>: <b><code>needs</code></b>: makes a job wait for the listed jobs to succeed. Jobs without <code>needs</code> start in parallel.</li><li><b>Concept</b>: <b><code>concurrency</code></b> + <code>group</code>: limits runs sharing a key. <b><code>strategy.matrix</code></b>: repeats a job with variants. <b><code>tools</code></b>: agent frontmatter, not Actions.</li><li><b>How it works</b>: <code>spec_analyzer</code> and <code>risk_reviewer</code> have no <code>needs</code>, so they run together. <code>plan_merger</code> with <code>needs</code> on both starts only when both are done.</li><li><b>Example</b>: <code>needs: [spec_analyzer, risk_reviewer]</code> on the merge job; <code>concurrency: group: multiagent-${{ github.ref }}</code> to avoid overlapping runs of the same branch.</li><li><b>Exam tip</b>: Words \"fan in\" or \"wait for\" mean <code>needs</code>. Pick YAML keys that really exist in Actions; <code>tools</code> is a decoy.</li></ul></div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/actions/using-jobs/using-concurrency\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Workflow concurrency groups</a></li></ul>"},
  {"n":53,"topic":7,"q":1,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  HOTSPOT<br />\nYou have a GitHub repository that uses GitHub Copilot Chat in Microsoft Visual Studio Code. Custom agents are stored in the repository under version control.<br />\nYour team uses a multi-agent workflow where a planner agent produces an implementation plan that is then handed off to an implementation agent to make changes.<br />\nRecent prompts cause the planner agent to start editing files and running commands before the plan is approved.<br />\nYou need to configure the planner agent to meet the following requirements:<br />\nUse only read-only tools.<br />\nHand off to the implementation agent only after the plan is approved.<br />\nHow should you configure the agent? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.<br />\n<br />\n<img title=\"image55\" src=\"images/image48.png\"><br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<span class=\"whead\">Proposed answers:</span><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"atbl\"><tr><th>#</th><th>Target</th><th>Value</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>tools: (dropdown)</td><td><b>['search','read','fetch']</b></td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>send: (dropdown)</td><td><b>false</b></td></tr></table></div><span class=\"whead\">Result:</span><pre class=\"acode\">name: planner-agent\ndescription: Planning\n<mark>tools: ['search','read','fetch']</mark>\nhandoffs:\n  - label: Start Implementation\n    agent: implementer\n    prompt: Now implement the plan outlined above.\n<mark>    send: false</mark></pre><span class=\"whead\">Why:</span><ul><li><b>['search','read','fetch']</b>: can only look things up, never change anything: read-only.</li><li><b>false</b>: the handoff stays a button, so a person clicks it after approving the plan.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Why not the others (options: ['edit','read','commit'], ['fetch','push','merge'], ['search','read','fetch'], ['search','read','publish'], false, true):</span><ul><li><b>['edit','read','commit']</b>: <code>edit</code> and <code>commit</code> write files, so the planner could change code before approval.</li><li><b>['fetch','push','merge']</b>: <code>push</code> and <code>merge</code> change the remote repository.</li><li><b>['search','read','publish']</b>: <code>publish</code> releases something, not read-only.</li><li><b>true</b>: as <code>send</code> it would hand off automatically before anyone approves.</li><li><b>false (for tools)</b>: not a tool list.</li></ul><div class=\"note\"><b>Note:</b> Both dropdowns show the same six choices; only the matching value makes sense for each blank.</div><div class=\"learnbox\"><span class=\"whead\">Learn it:</span><ul><li><b>Concept</b>: <b><code>tools</code></b> in agent frontmatter: the tools the agent may use. <code>search</code>, <code>read</code>, <code>fetch</code> only look at things; <code>edit</code>, <code>commit</code>, <code>push</code>, <code>merge</code>, <code>publish</code> change things.</li><li><b>Concept</b>: <b>Handoff <code>send</code></b>: <code>false</code> (default) waits for the user to click; <code>true</code> auto-submits.</li><li><b>How it works</b>: A planner with read-only tools cannot modify files. The handoff then appears as a button after the plan; the user reviews and clicks to pass it to the implementer.</li><li><b>Example</b>: <code>tools: ['search','read','fetch']</code> and <code>send: false</code>.</li><li><b>Exam tip</b>: \"Read-only\" means scan the list for any write verb; one is enough to rule it out. \"Only after approved\" means <code>send: false</code>.</li></ul></div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/agent-customization/custom-agents\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Custom agents in VS Code (incl. handoffs)</a></li></ul>"},
  {"n":54,"topic":7,"q":2,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  DRAG DROP<br><br>Existing Environment<br />\nGitHub Environment<br />\nThe GitHub environment contains the following:<br />\nThree repositories named product-api, billing-service, and infra-terraform.<br />\nBranch protection on the main branch in all repositories that requires at least one pull request review before merging<br />\nGitHub Actions runners used across all workflows<br />\nA GitHub team named SG_Dev that contains developers<br />\nA GitHub team named SG_Review that contains senior engineers and a security team<br />\nA .github/copilot-instructions.md file that includes general coding conventions for all features<br />\nAgent environment<br />\nThe product-api repository uses a GitHub Copilot coding agent named agent1 that has the following configurations:<br />\nNo custom agent profile is defined.<br />\nA Model Context Protocol (MCP) server named MCP1 is deployed to https://mcp.litwareinc.internal and provides access to internal ticketing and deployment APIs. MCP1 requires an API key for authentication.<br />\nA second Copilot coding agent named agent2 handles changes in infra-terraform and runs in parallel with agent1 when both agents have open assigned issues.<br />\nCopilot memory is NOT enabled for the organization.<br />\nProblem Statements<br />\nLitware identifies the following issues:<br />\nDuring two recent sessions, agent1 accessed files in billing-service, which is outside the agent's intended scope.<br />\nagent1 makes code changes immediately after receiving a task.<br />\nA developer named Ben, who is on the SG_Dev team, reports that agent1 completed a session with a successful status and opened a pull request, but the pull request contains no file changes. Other developers report this intermittently as well.<br />\nBoth agent1 and agent2 modified shared/config.yaml in a parallel test run, generating conflicting outputs.<br />\nagent1 consistently uses raw try-catch blocks for error handling, which violates the defined implementation guidelines of SG_Dev.<br />\nRequirements<br />\nPlanned Changes<br />\nLitware plans to make the following changes:<br />\nEnsure that agent1 can access all the tools in the environment.<br />\nProvide product-api with specific instructions to agent1 without affecting Copilot Chat or Copilot code review.<br />\nConfigure MCP1 as a tool for agent1 by modifying the product-api repository MCP configuration.<br />\nEnsure that Copilot retains details that it has learned and uses that knowledge for future work. This must be applied to all licensed members of the organization.<br />\nImplementation guidelines<br />\nThe development team at Litware identifies the following implementation guidelines:<br />\nAgent workflows must be able to run in parallel.<br />\nApplication error handling must use the repository ErrorHandler class.<br />\nagent1 and agent2 must run on isolated branches during parallel execution. File-level conflicts must be detected before merges, and both agents must be able to run concurrently.<br />\nSecurity requirements<br />\nLitware identifies the following security requirements:<br />\nOnly the members of SG_Review must be able to approve agent1 plan outputs.<br />\nAll API keys must be stored and accessed securely.<br />\nThe developers must NOT be able to self-approve.<br />\nAgent configuration<br />\n<img title=\"image57\" src=\"images/image10.png\"><br />\nYou need to implement the security requirements for agent1.<br />\nWhich three actions should you perform in sequence? To answer, move the appropriate actions from the list of actions to the answer area and arrange them in the correct order.<br />\n<img title=\"image58\" src=\"images/image50.png\"><br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<span class=\"whead\">Proposed answers:</span><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"atbl\"><tr><th>Step</th><th>Action (exact text)</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td><b>To the agent1 workflow, add a plan job that runs first and uploads the plan output as a workflow artifact.</b></td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td><b>In the product-api repository settings, create a GitHub Actions environment named plan-review and add SG_Review as the required reviewers.</b></td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td><b>Add an execute job that has needs: [plan] and environment: plan-review.</b></td></tr></table></div><span class=\"whead\">Result:</span><pre class=\"acode\">jobs:\n  plan:\n    # step 1: produces plan artifact\n  execute:\n<mark>    needs: [plan]</mark>\n<mark>    environment: plan-review   # step 2: SG_Review must approve</mark></pre><span class=\"whead\">Why:</span><ul><li><b>Plan first</b>: there must be a plan output to approve.</li><li><b>Environment with SG_Review</b>: pauses the job until only SG_Review members approve. That meets \"only SG_Review approves\".</li><li><b>Execute last</b>: <code>needs</code> orders it after the plan, <code>environment</code> makes it wait for approval.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Why not the other actions:</span><ul><li><b>Configure the plan job to advance to execution automatically if the job completes without any workflow errors.</b>: skips the human approval.</li><li><b>On the main branch of product-api, configure a branch protection ruleset that requires two approvals before agent1 can push changes.</b>: protects pushes to main, not plan outputs.</li><li><b>Add SG_Dev as additional required reviewers for the plan-review environment.</b>: developers must not approve; only SG_Review may.</li></ul><div class=\"learnbox\"><span class=\"whead\">Learn it:</span><ul><li><b>Concept</b>: <b>Environment</b>: a named deployment target in Actions that can require reviewers before jobs using it run.</li><li><b>Concept</b>: <b>Required reviewers</b>: people or teams who must approve; here only SG_Review, and you can prevent self-review.</li><li><b>Concept</b>: <b>Artifact</b>: a file saved by a workflow run (<code>upload-artifact</code>). <b><code>needs</code></b>: job order.</li><li><b>How it works</b>: A job with <code>environment: plan-review</code> pauses at \"waiting for review\" until a required reviewer approves, then runs. Create the environment in repo Settings, Environments.</li><li><b>Example</b>: Job <code>plan</code> uploads <code>plan.md</code>; job <code>execute</code> has <code>needs: [plan]</code> and <code>environment: plan-review</code>, so it only runs after SG_Review approves.</li><li><b>Exam tip</b>: For ordering questions, ask what must exist first: output to review, then the gate, then the job that uses the gate. Do not use the team that is not allowed to approve.</li></ul></div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/actions/managing-workflow-runs-and-deployments/managing-deployments/managing-environments-for-deployment\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Environments + required reviewers</a></li><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/coding-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot coding agent</a></li></ul>"},
  {"n":55,"topic":7,"q":3,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  Case study<br><br>Existing Environment<br />\nGitHub Environment<br />\nThe GitHub environment contains the following:<br />\nThree repositories named product-api, billing-service, and infra-terraform.<br />\nBranch protection on the main branch in all repositories that requires at least one pull request review before merging<br />\nGitHub Actions runners used across all workflows<br />\nA GitHub team named SG_Dev that contains developers<br />\nA GitHub team named SG_Review that contains senior engineers and a security team<br />\nA .github/copilot-instructions.md file that includes general coding conventions for all features<br />\nAgent environment<br />\nThe product-api repository uses a GitHub Copilot coding agent named agent1 that has the following configurations:<br />\nNo custom agent profile is defined.<br />\nA Model Context Protocol (MCP) server named MCP1 is deployed to https://mcp.litwareinc.internal and provides access to internal ticketing and deployment APIs. MCP1 requires an API key for authentication.<br />\nA second Copilot coding agent named agent2 handles changes in infra-terraform and runs in parallel with agent1 when both agents have open assigned issues.<br />\nCopilot memory is NOT enabled for the organization.<br />\nProblem Statements<br />\nLitware identifies the following issues:<br />\nDuring two recent sessions, agent1 accessed files in billing-service, which is outside the agent's intended scope.<br />\nagent1 makes code changes immediately after receiving a task.<br />\nA developer named Ben, who is on the SG_Dev team, reports that agent1 completed a session with a successful status and opened a pull request, but the pull request contains no file changes. Other developers report this intermittently as well.<br />\nBoth agent1 and agent2 modified shared/config.yaml in a parallel test run, generating conflicting outputs.<br />\nagent1 consistently uses raw try-catch blocks for error handling, which violates the defined implementation guidelines of SG_Dev.<br />\nRequirements<br />\nPlanned Changes<br />\nLitware plans to make the following changes:<br />\nEnsure that agent1 can access all the tools in the environment.<br />\nProvide product-api with specific instructions to agent1 without affecting Copilot Chat or Copilot code review.<br />\nConfigure MCP1 as a tool for agent1 by modifying the product-api repository MCP configuration.<br />\nEnsure that Copilot retains details that it has learned and uses that knowledge for future work. This must be applied to all licensed members of the organization.<br />\nImplementation guidelines<br />\nThe development team at Litware identifies the following implementation guidelines:<br />\nAgent workflows must be able to run in parallel.<br />\nApplication error handling must use the repository ErrorHandler class.<br />\nagent1 and agent2 must run on isolated branches during parallel execution. File-level conflicts must be detected before merges, and both agents must be able to run concurrently.<br />\nSecurity requirements<br />\nLitware identifies the following security requirements:<br />\nOnly the members of SG_Review must be able to approve agent1 plan outputs.<br />\nAll API keys must be stored and accessed securely.<br />\nThe developers must NOT be able to self-approve.<br />\nAgent configuration<br />\n<img title=\"image60\" src=\"images/image10.png\"><br />\nYou need to resolve the scoping issue associated to agent1.<br />\nWhat should you do?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tTo the profile of agent1, add a custom instruction specifying that the agent must NOT access billing-service or infra-terraform.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tAdd a permissions block to the agent1 workflow in product-api.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tCreate a fine-grained personal access token (PAT) scoped to product-api and store the PAT as a GitHub Actions secret for agent1 to use.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tCreate a ruleset for billing-service and infra-terraform that blocks push access from the github-actions bot account.<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<span class=\"whead\">Proposed answers:</span><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"atbl\"><tr><th>Question part</th><th>Answer</th></tr><tr><td>Correct answer</td><td><b>C. Create a fine-grained personal access token (PAT) scoped to product-api and store the PAT as a GitHub Actions secret for agent1 to use.</b></td></tr></table></div><span class=\"whead\">Why:</span><ul><li><b>Scoped token</b>: a fine-grained PAT limited to <code>product-api</code> cannot reach <code>billing-service</code> or <code>infra-terraform</code>.</li><li><b>Enforced</b>: access fails at authentication, whatever the agent tries.</li><li><b>Secret</b>: storing it as an Actions secret keeps the key safe.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Why not the others:</span><ul><li><b>A. To the profile of agent1, add a custom instruction specifying that the agent must NOT access billing-service or infra-terraform.</b>: only a request; the agent can still access the repos.</li><li><b>B. Add a permissions block to the agent1 workflow in product-api.</b>: scopes <code>GITHUB_TOKEN</code> for the workflow, not the agent's own credential.</li><li><b>D. Create a ruleset for billing-service and infra-terraform that blocks push access from the github-actions bot account.</b>: blocks pushes only; reading other repos still works.</li></ul><div class=\"note\"><b>Note:</b> Community answers are split between A and D, but the official answer is C.</div><div class=\"learnbox\"><span class=\"whead\">Learn it:</span><ul><li><b>Concept</b>: <b>Fine-grained PAT</b>: a personal access token limited to chosen repositories and permissions.</li><li><b>Concept</b>: <b><code>GITHUB_TOKEN</code> permissions</b>: the <code>permissions:</code> block limits the temporary workflow token, not other credentials.</li><li><b>Concept</b>: <b>Ruleset</b>: repo rules (such as blocking pushes). <b>Custom instruction</b>: guidance text the agent may follow, not enforced.</li><li><b>How it works</b>: Create the token with only <code>product-api</code> selected and needed permissions, then save it in repo Settings, Secrets as an Actions secret. Calls to other repos get denied.</li><li><b>Example</b>: Token scoped to <code>product-api</code> tries to read <code>billing-service</code>: GitHub returns 404/403.</li><li><b>Exam tip</b>: \"Scope / restrict access\" means limit the credential. Prose instructions and push-only rules do not stop reads.</li></ul></div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/authentication/keeping-your-account-and-data-secure/managing-your-personal-access-tokens\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Fine-grained personal access tokens</a></li><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/actions/security-for-github-actions/security-guides/automatic-token-authentication\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">GITHUB_TOKEN permissions</a></li></ul>"},
  {"n":56,"topic":7,"q":4,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  Case study<br><br>Overview<br />\nContoso Ltd. is a software development company located in the United States.<br />\nExisting Environment<br />\nGitHub Environment<br />\nContoso uses GitHub Enterprise and assigns GitHub Copilot Pro+ licenses to its developers. The developers use Microsoft Visual Studio Code as their IDE.<br />\nContoso has a customer portal. The code for the portal is stored in a GitHub repository named repo1 that contains the following:<br />\nA custom agent named agent1 that includes instructions to review specs related to best practices<br />\nA custom instruction file named validate-instructions.md that is used to validate tone of voice and applies to all .md and .txt files<br />\nA custom instruction file named codereview.instructions.md that is used by the Copilot coding agent but is excluded for use by the Copilot code review<br />\nrepo1 has the following structure:<br />\nThe front-end is stored in the /frontend folder.<br />\nThe API logic is stored in the /api folder.<br />\nContoso has a second repository named repo2 that contains a legacy .NET application named App1 built by using .NET 6. repo2 has a multi-agent workflow for modernization tasks.<br />\nContoso enables the Model Context Protocol (MCP) registry and allows the Microsoft Learn MCP Server. Every developer must configure their own connection to the Learn MCP Server.<br />\nProblem Statements<br />\nThe developers working in repo1 report that the Microsoft Learn documentation is NOT being retrieved when they attempt to validate a design by using agent1.<br />\nThe testing team at Contoso identifies that the customer portal uses inconsistent UI styles, which leads to customer confusion and branding issues. The UI inconsistencies stem from variations in the folder structure.<br />\n<br />\nAgent Logs<br />\nYou have the following logs for the multi-agent workflow used in repo2.<br />\n<img title=\"image61\" src=\"images/image1.png\"><br />\nRequirements<br />\nPlanned Changes<br />\nContoso plans to have all agents and developers in repo1 use the Microsoft Learn MCP to ensure that reviews are validated by using the appropriate documentation. This must be implemented centrally.<br />\nContoso plans to leverage AI-powered coding agents to implement new portal features and pages.<br />\nTechnical Requirements<br />\nApp1 must be upgraded to .NET 10. A previous upgrade attempt was started by using the Copilot modernization agent, but the attempt was never finalized.<br />\nYou plan to retry the upgrade. You must first analyze App1 by using AI, and then generate a report that contains breaking changes and deprecated patterns before retrying the upgrade.<br />\nAll AI-generated code for UI styling must adhere to a predefined folder structure.<br />\nThe architects at Contoso need help building implementation plans for repo1. The company wants to implement a new agent named agent2 to analyze the code base and the code requirements, and then respond with a detailed plan. The agent must NOT be able to edit files or run local commands.<br />\nThe developers must be able to delegate work to the Copilot coding agent by assigning issues to the agent.<br />\nWhile upgrading App1, the agent identifies 47 issues, including a security vulnerability, and 46 API incompatibilities across different projects.<br />\nWhich two actions are unsafe to delegate to the agent and require human involvement? Each correct answer presents a complete solution.<br><br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tValidate the assessment.md file for accuracy.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tGenerate the assessment.md file.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tApprove all Git commits.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tReview the plan.md file for dependencies.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>E.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tValidate whether the tasks.md file exists.<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<span class=\"whead\">Proposed answers:</span><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"atbl\"><tr><th>Question part</th><th>Answer</th></tr><tr><td>Correct answers</td><td><b>A. Validate the assessment.md file for accuracy.</b></td></tr><tr><td></td><td><b>C. Approve all Git commits.</b></td></tr></table></div><span class=\"whead\">Why:</span><ul><li><b>A. Validate the assessment.md file for accuracy.</b>: the report includes a security vulnerability; a person must confirm it is accurate.</li><li><b>C. Approve all Git commits.</b>: approval is the final gate before code lands; an accountable human must own it.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Why not the others:</span><ul><li><b>B. Generate the assessment.md file.</b>: writing the report is normal agent work.</li><li><b>D. Review the plan.md file for dependencies.</b>: analysis the agent can do safely.</li><li><b>E. Validate whether the tasks.md file exists.</b>: trivial check; no judgement needed.</li></ul><div class=\"learnbox\"><span class=\"whead\">Learn it:</span><ul><li><b>Concept</b>: <b>Human-in-the-loop</b>: a person checks and approves what AI produces when mistakes are costly.</li><li><b>Concept</b>: <b><code>assessment.md</code>, <code>plan.md</code>, <code>tasks.md</code></b>: files the modernization agent produces: findings, upgrade plan and task list.</li><li><b>Concept</b>: <b>Delegable work</b>: generating, analysing or checking files; results can be reviewed afterwards.</li><li><b>How it works</b>: The agent proposes (assessment, plan, commits). Findings involving security must be verified by someone accountable, and commits need human approval before they are accepted.</li><li><b>Example</b>: Agent reports 47 issues including a vulnerability: you confirm the vulnerability is real and approve the fix commits.</li><li><b>Exam tip</b>: Pick the actions about validating/approving; generate and \"check exists\" actions are safe to delegate.</li></ul></div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/porting/github-copilot-app-modernization/concepts\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">App modernization: assessment, plan, tasks.md</a></li><li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/porting/github-copilot-upgrade/overview\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot upgrade overview</a></li></ul>"},
  {"n":57,"topic":7,"q":5,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  DRAG DROP<br />\nYou have a GitHub repository that uses the GitHub Copilot CLI to run autonomous tasks.<br />\nYou need to validate each generated command before it runs. Any commands that attempt to modify paths outside the repository must be blocked.<br />\nHow should you complete the YAML? To answer, drag the appropriate values to the correct targets. Each value may be used once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.<br />\n<br />\n<img title=\"image62\" src=\"images/image52.png\"><br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<span class=\"whead\">Proposed answers:</span><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"atbl\"><tr><th>#</th><th>Target</th><th>Value</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>event: (box on the <code>event:</code> line)</td><td><b>pre-exec</b></td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>config: (box on the <code>config:</code> line under <code>cli: hooks:</code>)</td><td><b>.copilot/hooks.yaml</b></td></tr></table></div><span class=\"whead\">Result:</span><pre class=\"acode\">hooks:\n  - name: validate-generated-command\n<mark>    event: pre-exec</mark>\n    language: bash\n    ...\ncli:\n  hooks:\n    enabled: true\n<mark>    config: .copilot/hooks.yaml</mark></pre><span class=\"whead\">Why:</span><ul><li><b>pre-exec</b>: runs the check before the command executes, so <code>exit 1</code> can stop it.</li><li><b>.copilot/hooks.yaml</b>: is the file the Copilot CLI reads for its hooks (the file even names itself in line 1).</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Why not the others (available: .copilot/hooks.yaml, .github/workflows/hooks.yaml, false, post-exec, pre-exec, true):</span><ul><li><b>post-exec</b>: runs after the command; too late to block it.</li><li><b>.github/workflows/hooks.yaml</b>: a CI workflow path; the CLI does not read it.</li><li><b>true / false</b>: booleans, they fit neither box (<code>enabled: true</code> is already set).</li></ul><div class=\"learnbox\"><span class=\"whead\">Learn it:</span><ul><li><b>Concept</b>: <b>Hooks</b>: scripts that run at set events around the agent's actions to allow or block them.</li><li><b>Concept</b>: <b><code>pre-exec</code></b>: runs before a command; <b><code>post-exec</code></b>: after it.</li><li><b>Concept</b>: <b>Exit code</b>: <code>exit 1</code> (non-zero) means block; <code>exit 0</code> means allow.</li><li><b>How it works</b>: The hook script receives the generated command (here in <code>$COPILOT_COMMAND</code>), checks it with <code>grep</code>, and blocks it if it uses absolute paths or <code>../</code>. The <code>cli.hooks.config</code> key points to the hooks file.</li><li><b>Example</b>: The agent proposes <code>rm ../../data</code>; the hook matches <code>../</code>, prints a message and exits 1, so it never runs.</li><li><b>Exam tip</b>: \"Validate before it runs\" means <code>pre-exec</code>. The config path is the file named in the first comment line (<code># .copilot/hooks.yaml</code>).</li></ul></div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/copilot-cli\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot CLI</a></li></ul>"},
  {"n":58,"topic":7,"q":6,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You have a GitHub Enterprise Cloud Organization that uses GitHub Actions for CI/CD.<br />\nYou plan to enable an agent to update and deploy workflows that access production environment secrets.<br />\nYou need to select an autonomy level for a compliance-sensitive workflow.<br />\nWhich autonomy level should you select?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tread-only analysis with no ability to trigger workflows<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tautonomous execution limited to non-production environments<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\thuman-in-the-loop with required manual approvals for production<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tfully autonomous execution without approvals<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<span class=\"whead\">Proposed answers:</span><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"atbl\"><tr><th>Question part</th><th>Answer</th></tr><tr><td>Correct answer</td><td><b>C. human-in-the-loop with required manual approvals for production</b></td></tr></table></div><span class=\"whead\">Why:</span><ul><li><b>Production + secrets + compliance</b>: needs a person to approve before anything reaches production.</li><li><b>Agent still helps</b>: it prepares the workflow changes; humans approve the release.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Why not the others:</span><ul><li><b>A. read-only analysis with no ability to trigger workflows</b>: cannot update or deploy workflows at all.</li><li><b>B. autonomous execution limited to non-production environments</b>: excludes the production target that is required.</li><li><b>D. fully autonomous execution without approvals</b>: no approvals with production secrets breaks compliance.</li></ul><div class=\"learnbox\"><span class=\"whead\">Learn it:</span><ul><li><b>Concept</b>: <b>Read-only</b>: analyses only. <b>Non-production autonomy</b>: acts freely in dev/test only.</li><li><b>Concept</b>: <b>Human-in-the-loop</b>: agent prepares, humans approve key steps. <b>Fully autonomous</b>: no human gate.</li><li><b>Concept</b>: <b>Required reviewers on an environment</b>: the Actions feature that enforces manual approval for production.</li><li><b>How it works</b>: Define a <code>production</code> environment with required reviewers and (if needed) environment secrets. A job that uses it pauses until approved.</li><li><b>Example</b>: Agent edits the deploy workflow; the production job waits for the release team to approve.</li><li><b>Exam tip</b>: \"Compliance-sensitive\" plus \"production secrets\" always points to manual approval. The other choices either cannot do the job or remove control.</li></ul></div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/coding-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot coding agent</a></li></ul>"}
];